{"questions":[{"_i":260,"q":"Patient with suspected acute pancreatitis. Which enzyme has the HIGHEST specificity for pancreatitis?","a":["Lipase","Amylase","AST","Gamma-GT"],"ex":"Lipase is more specific for pancreatitis than amylase. Amylase also rises in renal insufficiency, salivary gland disorders, intestinal ischemia and other abdominal disorders. Lipase remains elevated longer (3-5 days vs. 1-2 days for amylase).","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Lipase is more specific for pancreatitis than amylase — amylase also rises in renal insufficiency, salivary gland disorders and intestinal ischemia. Lipase remains elevated longer (3-5 days vs 1-2 days).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Lipase specificity","desc":"Almost exclusively pancreas-specific. Rises 3-6 h after onset, peaks at 24 h, normal after 3-5 days."},{"title":"Amylase low specificity","desc":"Present in salivary glands, intestine, gonads, kidneys → elevated in many other causes. Normalizes faster than lipase."},{"title":"Diagnostic criteria","desc":"≥2 of 3: (1) typical epigastric pain, (2) lipase/amylase >3×ULN, (3) CT images compatible with pancreatitis."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Gallstone pancreatitis","desc":"ALT >3×ULN strongly supports gallstone pancreatitis as cause. ERCP + sphincterotomy with biliary obstruction."},{"label":"Alcohol pancreatitis","desc":"Recurrent episodes with alcohol use. Risk of chronic pancreatitis."},{"label":"Severe pancreatitis","desc":"CRP >150 after 48 h, APACHE II score, CT severity index — necrosis formation in 20%. Mortality 30-40%."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Aggressive IV fluid resuscitation (Lactated Ringer 250-500 mL/h in first hours) — cornerstone of treatment.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Nutrition","detail":"Early enteral nutrition (<48 h) via nasogastric tube in severe pancreatitis — reduces complications. No TPN unless enteral nutrition is impossible."},{"naam":"Antibiotics","detail":"Prophylactic not recommended in pancreatitis. Yes in infected necrosis (FNA-guided culture, carbapenems)."},{"naam":"ERCP","detail":"In gallstone pancreatitis with cholangitis or persistent biliary obstruction: ERCP + sphincterotomy <24-72 h."}]},{"_i":261,"q":"48-year-old man, increased thirst and urine production, tired, fasting blood glucose 8.4 mmol/L and 9.1 mmol/L on repeat. HbA1c 62 mmol/mol. Diagnosis?","a":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Primary hyperaldosteronism"],"ex":"Type 2 DM diagnosis: ≥2 fasting glucose values ≥7.0 mmol/L, or HbA1c ≥53 mmol/mol, or random glucose ≥11.1 mmol/L + symptoms. Type 2 occurs in adults with risk factors (overweight, family history).","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Type 2 DM: relative insulin deficiency due to insulin resistance + progressive beta-cell loss. Prevalence 400 million worldwide.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"HbA1c","desc":"glycated hemoglobin, reflects average blood glucose over 2-3 months."},{"title":"Diagnostic","desc":"≥53 mmol/mol (7%)."},{"title":"OGTT (oral glucose tolerance test)","desc":"2 h value ≥11.1 mmol/L after 75 g glucose = DM."},{"title":"Prediabetes","desc":"fasting 6.1-6.9 or HbA1c 42-52."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Type 1 DM","desc":"younger age, lean, autoantibodies (GAD65, IA-2, ZnT8), low C-peptide."},{"label":"LADA","desc":"slowly progressive autoimmune, adults."},{"label":"DI","desc":"polydipsia but normal glucose; diagnosed with water deprivation test."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"DM2 step 1: metformin + lifestyle.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"add SGLT2 inhibitor (with CVD/kidney disease) or GLP-1 agonist (with obesity)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Insulin if HbA1c persistently high."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Treatment target HbA1c <53 mmol/mol (individualize)."}]},{"_i":262,"q":"35-year-old woman, palpitations, weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, bulging eyes (exophthalmos). TSH <0.01 mIU/L, free T4 elevated. Diagnosis?","a":["Graves' disease","Toxic multinodular goiter","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Subacute thyroiditis"],"ex":"Graves' disease: the most common cause of hyperthyroidism in younger women. Characteristic: hyperthyroidism + exophthalmos (Graves ophthalmopathy) + sometimes pretibial myxedema. TSI (thyrotropin-stimulating immunoglobulins) are pathognomonic.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Hyperthyroidism: overproduction of thyroid hormone. Graves is autoimmune (TSI stimulate TSH receptor).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Symptoms","desc":"tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, weight loss, heat intolerance, sweating, tremor, diarrhea, menstrual disorders."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"TSH is the most sensitive screening test."},{"title":"Treatment","desc":"thionamides (thiamazole, propylthiouracil), radioactive iodine, surgery."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hashimoto","desc":"autoimmune hypothyroidism (TPO-Ab+), initially transient hyperthyroidism (hashitoxicosis)."},{"label":"Subacute (De Quervain)","desc":"painful thyroid, self-limiting."},{"label":"Toxic adenoma","desc":"solitary hot nodule on scintigraphy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Graves: thiamazole 1-2 years → 50% recurrence → then radioactive iodine or thyroidectomy.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Propranolol for symptom control."},{"naam":"Graves ophthalmopathy","detail":"rapidly remitting hyperthyroidism, selenium, corticosteroids in severe exophthalmos."}]},{"_i":263,"q":"55-year-old man, tired, cold, slow, constipation, bradycardia, dry skin, elevated TSH (28 mIU/L), low free T4. Diagnosis?","a":["Primary hypothyroidism","Secondary hypothyroidism","Cushing's disease","Primary hyperaldosteronism"],"ex":"Primary hypothyroidism: high TSH (pituitary compensates) + low free T4. The most common cause in Western countries is Hashimoto thyroiditis (autoimmune, TPO antibodies positive). Secondary hypothyroidism: low TSH + low T4 (pituitary problem).","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Hypothyroidism: insufficient thyroid hormone synthesis. Symptoms: fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, bradycardia, delayed reflexes, dry skin, hair loss, cognitive slowing.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"TSH","desc":"negative feedback with T4."},{"title":"In primary hypothyroidism","desc":"T4 low → TSH rises (compensatory)."},{"title":"Hashimoto","desc":"TPO antibodies destroy follicular cells."},{"title":"Treatment","desc":"levothyroxine (L-T4) orally, dose titration based on TSH."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Secondary hypothyroidism (pituitary)","desc":"TSH low/normal + T4 low → pituitary MRI."},{"label":"Tertiary","desc":"TRH deficiency (hypothalamic)."},{"label":"Sick euthyroid syndrome","desc":"T3 low in severe illness but TSH normal."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Levothyroxine (L-T4): start low and titrate, target TSH 0.5-2.5 mIU/L.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"With cardiac comorbidity","detail":"titrate slowly."},{"naam":"Myxedema coma","detail":"IV L-T4 + hydrocortisone + intensive care."}]},{"_i":264,"q":"42-year-old woman, months of fatigue, skin pigmentation (including mucous membranes), weight loss, hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia. Diagnosis?","a":["Primary adrenocortical insufficiency (Addison's disease)","Secondary adrenocortical insufficiency","Hypothyroidism","SIADH"],"ex":"Addison's disease: autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex → deficiency of cortisol and aldosterone. Hyperpigmentation (due to ACTH increase) is characteristic of primary adrenocortical insufficiency and absent in secondary. Hyponatremia + hyperkalemia = aldosterone deficiency.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Primary adrenocortical insufficiency (Addison): deficiency of glucocorticoid (cortisol) + mineralocorticoid (aldosterone). Cause: autoimmune (70%), tuberculosis, adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen in meningococcal sepsis).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Cortisol deficiency."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"ACTH increase (negative feedback lost)."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"MSH byproduct."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"hyperpigmentation. Aldosterone deficiency."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"sodium loss + potassium retention + volume depletion."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Option 1","desc":"Secondary adrenocortical insufficiency (pituitary ACTH deficiency): no hyperpigmentation (ACTH low), no aldosterone deficiency (RAAS intact)."},{"label":"SIADH","desc":"hyponatremia but no potassium-handling abnormality or hypotension."},{"label":"Hypothyroidism","desc":"also fatigue but no hyperpigmentation."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Hydrocortisone 2-3× daily (mimics physiological pattern).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Fludrocortisone for mineralocorticoid replacement."},{"naam":"Addison crisis","detail":"IV hydrocortisone 100 mg bolus + 200 mg/24 h + NaCl 0.9% resuscitation."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Always provide SOS injection."}]},{"_i":265,"q":"38-year-old man, severe hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis. Aldosterone high, renin low. Diagnosis?","a":["Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome)","Renovascular hypertension","Cushing syndrome","Pheochromocytoma"],"ex":"Primary hyperaldosteronism: autonomous aldosterone production independent of the renin-angiotensin system. Classic: hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis + high aldosterone + low renin. Cause: aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn) or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Primary hyperaldosteronism is the most common cause of secondary hypertension (5-10% of hypertensive patients). Aldosterone → Na retention + K loss → hypertension + hypokalemia.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Aldosterone stimulates ENaC (epithelial sodium channel) in distal kidney."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Na reabsorption + K/H excretion."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis. Hypokalemia: muscle weakness, polyuria (nephrogenic DI due to hypokalemia)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Renovascular hypertension","desc":"also secondary hypertension but high renin + high aldosterone."},{"label":"Cushing","desc":"high cortisol, buffalo hump, striae."},{"label":"Pheochromocytoma","desc":"episodic, catecholamines elevated."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Aldosterone-producing adenoma: laparoscopic adrenalectomy.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Bilateral hyperplasia","detail":"mineralocorticoid antagonist (spironolactone or eplerenone)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Temporary potassium supplementation."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Blood pressure control."}]},{"_i":266,"q":"30-year-old woman, obese, high blood pressure, purple striae on abdomen, buffalo hump, moon-shaped face, muscle atrophy. 24-hour urinary free cortisol elevated. Diagnosis?","a":["Cushing syndrome","Primary hyperaldosteronism","Hypothyroidism","Polycystic ovary syndrome"],"ex":"Cushing syndrome: chronic exposure to glucocorticoids (endogenous or exogenous). Characteristic: central obesity, purple striae, buffalo hump, moon-shaped face, hypertension, muscle atrophy. Most common cause: exogenous corticosteroid use.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Endogenous Cushing: (1) Cushing disease (ACTH-producing pituitary adenoma, 70%), (2) ectopic ACTH production (lung carcinoma), (3) primary adrenocortical adenoma/carcinoma. Diagnostic algorithm: screening (24 h UFC, late-night salivary cortisol, overnight dexamethasone suppression test) → determine cause (ACTH, MRI/CT, CRH test).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Cortisol","desc":"protein catabolism (muscle atrophy, striae), fat redistribution (central), gluconeogenesis (hyperglycemia), immunosuppression, mineralocorticoid effect (hypertension, hypokalemia)."},{"title":"Purple striae","desc":"stretched skin + catabolism."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Metabolic syndrome","desc":"obesity but no striae/muscle atrophy, normal cortisol."},{"label":"Pseudo-Cushing","desc":"depression/alcoholism raises cortisol but dexamethasone suppression test normal."},{"label":"PCOS","desc":"also androgenic features and cycle disorders but no true Cushing features."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Cushing disease: transsphenoidal pituitary adenoma resection.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Adrenal adenoma","detail":"adrenalectomy."},{"naam":"Ectopic","detail":"treat primary tumor."},{"naam":"Medication bridge","detail":"metyrapone, ketoconazole."}]},{"_i":267,"q":"By which mechanism does metformin lower blood glucose in type 2 diabetes?","a":["Inhibition of hepatic gluconeogenesis via AMPK activation","Stimulation of insulin secretion by beta cells","Inhibition of intestinal glucose reabsorption via SGLT2","Increased insulin sensitivity via PPARγ activation"],"ex":"Metformin activates AMPK (AMP-activated protein kinase) → inhibition of hepatic gluconeogenesis → liver glucose output decreases. Metformin does not stimulate insulin secretion and therefore does not cause hypoglycemia as monotherapy.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Metformin activates AMPK → inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis → less liver glucose output. No hypoglycemia risk as monotherapy. First choice in type 2 DM (UKPDS: cardiovascular benefit demonstrated).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"AMPK activation","desc":"Inhibition of mitochondrial complex I → less ATP → AMPK active → inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis. Reduces insulin resistance in muscle and liver."},{"title":"No insulin secretion","desc":"Metformin does not stimulate insulin — therefore no hypoglycemia as monotherapy and no weight gain."},{"title":"Lactic acidosis","desc":"Rare but severe adverse effect in renal insufficiency (eGFR <30) — metformin accumulation → lactate production. Stop with eGFR <30 or contrast nephropathy risk."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Metformin (first choice)","desc":"No hypoglycemia, weight-neutral, inexpensive, cardiovascular benefit. Stop with eGFR <30."},{"label":"SGLT2 inhibitors","desc":"Cardioprotective (HFrEF), nephroprotective. Add in DM2 + heart failure or kidney disease."},{"label":"GLP-1 agonists (semaglutide)","desc":"Weight loss + cardiovascular benefit. Add in DM2 + obesity or atherosclerosis."},{"label":"Sulfonylureas","desc":"Stimulate insulin secretion → hypoglycemia risk + weight gain. Less preferred."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"DKA in DM2: IV insulin + fluids + electrolyte correction (monitor K+).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Start metformin","detail":"500 mg with the evening meal, increase to 1000 mg 2×/day over 4 weeks (GI adverse effects reduced with slow titration)."},{"naam":"Step 2 (if HbA1c >53)","detail":"SGLT2 inhibitor (empagliflozin/dapagliflozin) with CVD/HF/kidney disease, or GLP-1 agonist with obesity."},{"naam":"Insulin","detail":"Add with persistent hyperglycemia despite 2-3 oral agents. Basal insulin (glargine) 1×/day 10 U as starting dose."}]},{"_i":268,"q":"52-year-old man, broad hands and feet, enlarged head, shoes fit more loosely. High IGF-1 level. Diagnosis?","a":["Acromegaly","Gigantism","Hyperthyroidism","Primary hyperparathyroidism"],"ex":"Acromegaly: overproduction of growth hormone (GH) by a pituitary adenoma after epiphyseal plate closure → enlargement of hands, feet and skull (not height). Elevated IGF-1 is the best screening test. Gigantism occurs before closure of the growth plates.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Acromegaly: insidious onset, diagnosis often 10+ years after onset. Symptoms: enlargement of hands/feet, prognathism, diastema, carpal tunnel syndrome, sleep apnea, DM, colon polyps, cardiomegaly.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"GH adenoma."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"GH stimulates liver cells to produce IGF-1."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"IGF-1 stimulates tissues. Confirmation: GH suppression after OGTT (normal <1 μg/L; in acromegaly: no suppression). Pituitary MRI: macroadenoma or microadenoma."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Gigantism","desc":"acromegaly before puberty → excessive linear growth."},{"label":"Hyperthyroidism","desc":"hypermetabolism but no skeletal changes."},{"label":"Hemimegaly","desc":"unilateral."},{"label":"Pachydermoperiostosis","desc":"benign."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Transsphenoidal adenoma resection (first choice).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Medication in residual disease","detail":"somatostatin analogues (octreotide, lanreotide), pegvisomant (GH receptor antagonist)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Radiosurgery as adjunct."}]},{"_i":269,"q":"In type 1 diabetes mellitus, insulin therapy is optional; with diet and oral antidiabetic drugs, blood glucose can also be well controlled.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. In type 1 DM there is an absolute insulin deficiency due to autoimmune beta-cell destruction. Insulin is life-saving — without insulin, diabetic ketoacidosis occurs. Oral antidiabetic drugs are ineffective when beta-cell function is absent.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"In type 1 DM there is an absolute insulin deficiency due to autoimmune beta-cell destruction. Insulin is life-saving — without insulin, DKA occurs. Oral antidiabetic drugs are ineffective without functional beta cells.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Autoimmune beta-cell destruction","desc":"Antibodies (GAD65, IA-2, ZnT8) + cytotoxic T cells destroy beta cells → no insulin production."},{"title":"DKA mechanism","desc":"Insulin deficiency → glucagon dominant → lipolysis → free fatty acids to liver → ketone formation (beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate) → acidosis."},{"title":"HHS in DM2","desc":"Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic syndrome in type 2 DM — no ketosis (still some insulin), but severe hyperglycemia + dehydration."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Type 1 DM","desc":"Young, lean, acute onset (DKA), GAD antibodies positive, C-peptide low. Lifelong insulin."},{"label":"LADA","desc":"Adult, gradual onset, initially resembles DM2, GAD antibodies positive. Insulin-dependent within years."},{"label":"Type 2 DM","desc":"Older, overweight, familial, autoantibodies negative, C-peptide normal/high. Oral agents initially."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"DKA: IV insulin + NaCl 0.9% 1 L/h initially + K+ correction (K+ >3.5 before insulin start). Do NOT stop insulin.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Basal-bolus regimen","detail":"Long-acting insulin (glargine/degludec) 1×/day + rapid-acting insulin (aspart/lispro) before each meal. Carbohydrate counting for bolus calculation."},{"naam":"Insulin pump (CSII)","detail":"Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion + Flash glucose monitoring (FGM/CGM). Better HbA1c in motivated patients."},{"naam":"Hypoglycemia","detail":"Mild: sugar/glucose tablets. Severe: glucagon IM or dextrose 50% IV. Patient + family education mandatory."}]},{"_i":270,"q":"An elevated TSH in a pregnant woman in the first trimester is normal and requires no treatment.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. An elevated TSH in a pregnant woman can indicate hypothyroidism, which is associated with miscarriage, pregnancy complications and impaired neurocognitive development of the child. Treatment with levothyroxine is indicated. (NB: TSH reference values fall in the first trimester due to the hCG effect.).","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Thyroid hormone is crucial for fetal neuronal migration in the first trimester. TSH decreases physiologically (hCG effect), but elevated TSH is abnormal and requires treatment to prevent fetal brain damage.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Physiological TSH decrease","desc":"hCG has structural similarity to TSH → stimulates TSH receptor → T4 increase → TSH decrease. Normal: TSH <2.5 mIU/L in trimester 1."},{"title":"Fetal dependence","desc":"Fetus only produces its own thyroid hormone after 12 weeks — before that fully dependent on mother. Hypothyroidism → severe neurological deficit."},{"title":"Elevated TSH in trimester 1","desc":"Gives increased risk of miscarriage, preterm birth, lower child IQ. Treatment: start levothyroxine immediately."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hypothyroidism in pregnancy","desc":"TSH elevated, free T4 low or normal. Levothyroxine immediately. Dose requirement rises in pregnancy."},{"label":"Hyperthyroidism (Graves)","desc":"TSH low, T4 high, TRAb positive. PTU in trimester 1 (thiamazole teratogenic), then switch to thiamazole."},{"label":"Gestational hyperthyroidism","desc":"Due to hCG stimulation in first trimester — physiological. TSH slightly low but T4 normal. No treatment needed."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start levothyroxine immediately with TSH >2.5 mIU/L in trimester 1 — do not wait.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Levothyroxine dose","detail":"Start 50-100 mcg/day. Check TSH every 4 weeks in first half of pregnancy. Dose rises on average 25-50% in pregnancy."},{"naam":"Target","detail":"TSH <2.5 mIU/L (trimester 1) or <3 mIU/L (trimester 2-3). Free T4 normal."},{"naam":"Postpartum","detail":"After delivery, reduce dose back to pre-pregnancy dose. Check TSH after 6 weeks."}]},{"_i":271,"q":"Hypercalcemia in a patient with malignancy is almost always due to bone metastases.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Hypercalcemia in malignancy can also arise from humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy (HHM): tumor production of PTHrP (PTH-related protein), which activates the same receptor as PTH. HHM is responsible for 80% of malignant hypercalcemia, bone metastases for ~20%.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Hypercalcemia in malignancy is in 80% due to PTHrP (humoral hypercalcemia), not bone metastases (20%). PTHrP binds PTH receptor → bone resorption + renal calcium retention → hypercalcemia while PTH itself is low.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"PTHrP mechanism","desc":"Tumor produces PTH-related protein → binds PTH receptor → bone resorption via osteoclasts + renal Ca reabsorption → hypercalcemia. PTH itself low (negative feedback)."},{"title":"Causes of hypercalcemia","desc":"(1) Primary HPT: PTH high (outpatient). (2) Malignancy: PTHrP or metastases (hospital). (3) Sarcoidosis/vitD toxicity: 1,25-OHD high. (4) FHH: familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia."},{"title":"Symptoms","desc":""Bones, stones, groans, psychic moans": bone pain, kidney stones, constipation/nausea, depression/psychosis/confusion. With Ca >3.5: renal insufficiency, cardiac arrhythmias."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Malignancy (PTHrP)","desc":"PTH low, PTHrP high, rapid onset, severe hypercalcemia. Squamous cell ca, RCC, breast ca, myeloma."},{"label":"Primary hyperparathyroidism","desc":"PTH high (despite high calcium), asymptomatic or kidney stones. Adenoma 80%. Surgery if symptomatic."},{"label":"Sarcoidosis","desc":"1,25-OHD high (macrophages activate vitamin D), elevated ACE, lymphadenopathy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Severe hypercalcemia (Ca >3.0): IV NaCl 0.9% 200-300 mL/h + furosemide after fluid resuscitation.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Bisphosphonates","detail":"Zoledronate 4 mg IV once — inhibits osteoclasts, effect after 2-4 days, works 3-4 weeks. First choice in malignant hypercalcemia."},{"naam":"Denosumab","detail":"Anti-RANK-L antibody — effective in bisphosphonate-refractory hypercalcemia or renal insufficiency."},{"naam":"Treat cause","detail":"In malignancy: treat tumor. In primary HPT: parathyroidectomy curative."}]},{"_i":272,"q":"SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) are contraindicated in patients with type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease because they worsen kidney function.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) are actually nephroprotective in type 2 DM + chronic kidney disease: they reduce glomerular hyperfiltration and lower the risk of kidney disease progression. They are contraindicated with eGFR <20-30 (insufficient glucosuria effect).","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"SGLT2 inhibitors are actually nephroprotective in DM2 + chronic kidney disease. They reduce glomerular hyperfiltration via tubuloglomerular feedback. They are contraindicated with eGFR <20-30 (glucosuria effect insufficient).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"SGLT2 blockade","desc":"Less sodium reabsorption in proximal tubule → more sodium at macula densa → tubuloglomerular feedback → afferent arteriole constriction → reduced glomerular pressure → nephroprotection."},{"title":"Cardioprotection","desc":"EMPA-REG/DECLARE: empagliflozin/dapagliflozin reduce heart failure hospitalizations in DM2 + CVD. Effect partly glucose-independent (preload reduction, ketones as energy source)."},{"title":"Adverse effects","desc":"Urogenital infections (glucosuria creates sugar-rich urine). Euglycemic DKA (rare, cave perioperatively). Stop 3-4 days before surgery."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"SGLT2 inhibitors (nephroprotective)","desc":"Stop with eGFR <20-30. Cardioprotective in HFrEF (also without diabetes). Glucosuria effect disappears at low eGFR."},{"label":"GLP-1 agonists","desc":"Weight loss + cardiovascular benefit in atherosclerosis. No direct kidney effect. Subcutaneous injection."},{"label":"ACE inhibitors/ARB","desc":"Also nephroprotective via reduction of intraglomerular pressure — combine with SGLT2 inhibitor in DM2 nephropathy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Perioperatively: stop SGLT2 inhibitor 3-4 days before surgery (euglycemic DKA prevention).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"DM2 + chronic kidney disease","detail":"Empagliflozin/dapagliflozin first choice (alongside metformin) if eGFR ≥20 — slow kidney failure progression (DAPA-CKD, EMPA-KIDNEY)."},{"naam":"DM2 + heart failure (HFrEF)","detail":"Dapagliflozin reduces hospitalizations and mortality. Indication independent of DM diagnosis (DAPA-HF study)."},{"naam":"Monitoring","detail":"Check eGFR before start and periodically. Stop with eGFR <20. Watch for urogenital infections."}]},{"_i":273,"q":"Which feature distinguishes primary from secondary adrenocortical insufficiency?","a":["Hyperpigmentation of skin and mucous membranes","Hypotension","Hyponatremia","Fatigue and weakness"],"ex":"Hyperpigmentation is specific for primary adrenocortical insufficiency (Addison). ACTH rises compensatorily → MSH (melanocyte-stimulating hormone) as byproduct → melanin stimulation. In secondary insufficiency, ACTH is low → no pigmentation. Hypotension, hyponatremia and fatigue occur in both.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Hyperpigmentation is pathognomonic for primary adrenocortical insufficiency (Addison). High ACTH stimulates MSH receptors on melanocytes. In secondary insufficiency, ACTH is low — no pigmentation.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"ACTH-MSH axis","desc":"POMC (ACTH precursor) split into ACTH + MSH. MSH binds MC1R on melanocytes → melanin. Locations: sun-exposed skin, palms, scars, gums, buccal mucosa."},{"title":"Primary insufficiency","desc":"Adrenal gland damaged (autoimmune in 70%, tuberculosis, hemorrhage) → cortisol + aldosterone deficiency → hyponatremia + hyperkalemia + hypotension. ACTH high as compensation."},{"title":"Secondary insufficiency","desc":"Pituitary/hypothalamus problem → ACTH low → cortisol deficiency but aldosterone normal (renin-angiotensin intact). No pigmentation, no hyperkalemia."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Primary (Addison)","desc":"ACTH high, cortisol low, aldosterone low, hyperpigmentation, hyponatremia + hyperkalemia. Autoantibodies (21-OH-Ab)."},{"label":"Secondary (pituitary)","desc":"ACTH low, cortisol low, aldosterone normal, no pigmentation, no hyperkalemia. Cause: tumor, radiation, Sheehan."},{"label":"Iatrogenic (steroid withdrawal)","desc":"After sudden discontinuation of long-term corticosteroids — secondary type (HPA axis suppressed). Never stop abruptly after >3 weeks of use."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Addison crisis: hydrocortisone 100 mg IV bolus → 200 mg/24 h continuous IV + NaCl 0.9% 1 L over 30-60 min.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Maintenance therapy","detail":"Hydrocortisone 15-25 mg/day (higher morning dose to mimic circadian rhythm) + fludrocortisone 0.05-0.2 mg/day (mineralocorticoid)."},{"naam":"Sick-day rule","detail":"With fever/infection: increase hydrocortisone 2-3×. With vomiting: parenteral administration. Patient gets emergency injection instructions."},{"naam":"ACTH stimulation test","detail":"Cortisol after 250 mcg ACTH <550 nmol/L after 30 min = adrenocortical insufficiency."}]},{"_i":274,"q":"28-year-old patient, oligomenorrhea, acne, hirsutism, overweight, polycystic ovaries on ultrasound. LH/FSH ratio elevated. Diagnosis?","a":["Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Hyperprolactinemia","Primary ovarian insufficiency"],"ex":"PCOS is the most common endocrine disorder in women of reproductive age (5-10%). Rotterdam criteria (≥2 of 3): oligo-/anovulation, hyperandrogenism (clinical or biochemical), polycystic ovaries on ultrasound.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"PCOS: chronic anovulation + hyperandrogenism. Insulin resistance plays a central role (also in lean PCOS patients).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"LH/FSH ratio >2.5 in PCOS: increased LH pulsatility."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"more androgen stimulation of theca cells."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"testosterone/DHEAS rises."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"peripheral aromatization to estrogen. Overweight worsens insulin resistance."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"hyperandrogenism."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (late-onset)","desc":"17-OHP elevated after ACTH stimulation."},{"label":"Hyperprolactinemia","desc":"galactorrhea, prolactin high, pituitary MRI."},{"label":"POI (premature ovarian insufficiency)","desc":"FSH high, estradiol low, <40 years."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Fertility desire: ovulation induction (clomiphene, letrozole), metformin.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"No pregnancy desire","detail":"combined oral contraceptive (androgen-antagonistic: cyproterone acetate)."},{"naam":"Lifestyle","detail":"weight reduction improves cycle and insulin sensitivity."}]},{"_i":275,"q":"60-year-old man, kidney stones, osteoporosis, mild hypercalcemia. PTH elevated. Calcium and PTH elevated on repeated measurement. Diagnosis?","a":["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Malignant hypercalcemia","Sarcoidosis","Vitamin D toxicity"],"ex":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: autonomous overproduction of PTH by a parathyroid adenoma (85%) or hyperplasia. Characteristic: high PTH + high calcium (normally: negative feedback suppresses PTH in hypercalcemia). Most common cause of hypercalcemia in the outpatient clinic.","dl":"Endocrinology","wiki_kern":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: PTH stimulates bone resorption + renal calcium reabsorption + 1-alpha-hydroxylase (more active vitamin D) → hypercalcemia + hyperphosphaturia (low phosphate). Kidney stones (calcium oxalate/phosphate), osteitis fibrosa cystica, osteoporosis.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Sestamibi scintigraphy or neck ultrasound: localization of parathyroid adenoma."},{"title":"Laboratory","desc":"high PTH, high calcium, low phosphate, high urinary calcium."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Differentiation from malignant hypercalcemia: PTH low in malignancy (PTHrP not detectable in PTH assay, but separate assay available)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH)","desc":"high calcium, high PTH, but low urinary calcium (calcium/creatinine ratio <0.01) → no surgery needed."},{"label":"Secondary HPT","desc":"with renal insufficiency or vit."},{"label":"Option 3","desc":"D deficiency → high PTH but low/normal calcium."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Asymptomatic mild: follow-up (calcium, eGFR, bone density).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Surgical indications","detail":"calcium >2.85 mmol/L, eGFR <60, osteoporosis (T-score <-2.5), age <50 years."},{"naam":"Surgery","detail":"parathyroidectomy (curative in adenoma >95%)."}]},{"_i":276,"q":"70-year-old man, oliguria 2 days after heart surgery. Creatinine increased from 90 to 310 μmol/L. Urine sodium concentration 12 mmol/L, urine osmolality 580 mOsm/kg. Most likely cause?","a":["Prerenal acute kidney injury","Renal acute kidney injury (ATN)","Postrenal acute kidney injury","Contrast nephropathy"],"ex":"Prerenal kidney failure: low urine Na (<20) + high osmolality (>500) = intact tubular system responding to volume depletion. ATN: urine Na >40 + low osmolality.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Acute kidney injury (AKI): prerenal (60%, reduced perfusion), renal (35%, acute tubular necrosis (ATN) most frequent), postrenal (5%, obstruction). KDIGO: creatinine rise ≥1.5× within 7 days or oliguria <0.5 mL/kg/h >6 hours.","wiki_redflag":"NSAIDs + ACE inhibitors with volume depletion → rapid progression to oliguria and dialysis-requiring kidney failure. Stop both immediately in acute kidney injury.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Fractional sodium excretion (FENa)","desc":"FENa = (urine-Na × serum-Cr) / (serum-Na × urine-Cr) × 100%. FENa <1% = prerenal; FENa >2% = renal (ATN)."},{"title":"FENa limitation","desc":"Unreliable with use of diuretics, contrast nephropathy or rhabdomyolysis."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Prerenal AKI","desc":"Recovers quickly after volume correction — no tubular damage. Urine Na low, osmolality high."},{"label":"ATN (renal)","desc":"Tubules damaged → granular ('muddy brown') casts in urine sediment. Recovery in 1-3 weeks."},{"label":"Interstitial nephritis","desc":"After medication (NSAIDs, antibiotics): eosinophilia, rash, eosinophiluria — stop causal agent."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Prerenal: fluid supplementation + discontinue nephrotoxins (NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, contrast).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"ATN","detail":"Supportive — maintain diuresis, electrolyte control, nutrition."},{"naam":"Dialysis indications","detail":"Refractory hyperkalemia, uremic encephalopathy/pericarditis, acidosis (pH <7.1), fluid overload."}]},{"_i":277,"q":"25-year-old woman, edema of entire body, proteinuria 6 g/24 h, hypoalbuminemia (20 g/L), hypercholesterolemia. Blood pressure normal. Diagnosis?","a":["Nephrotic syndrome","Nephritic syndrome","Heart failure","Liver cirrhosis"],"ex":"Nephrotic syndrome: proteinuria >3.5 g/24 h + hypoalbuminemia + edema + hypercholesterolemia — without hematuria.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Nephrotic syndrome: podocyte damage → loss of glomerular barrier → massive protein excretion. Causes: minimal change disease (children, 90%), membranous nephropathy (adults), diabetic nephropathy.","wiki_redflag":"Renal vein thrombosis in nephrotic syndrome: loss of antithrombin III via urine → hypercoagulable. Think of flank pain + acute GFR decline — immediate imaging.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Cascade","desc":"Podocyte damage → basement membrane permeable → massive proteinuria → hypoalbuminemia → oncotic pressure falls → edema. Liver compensates with lipoprotein production → hypercholesterolemia."},{"title":"Thrombosis risk","desc":"Loss of antithrombin III, protein C and S → increased risk of deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism and renal vein thrombosis."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Heart failure","desc":"Edema + elevated central venous pressure + dyspnea — proteinuria mild."},{"label":"Liver cirrhosis","desc":"Ascites dominant, splenomegaly, abnormal liver enzymes — albumin falls due to liver failure."},{"label":"Nephritic syndrome","desc":"Hematuria + RBC casts + hypertension — proteinuria less extreme."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Minimal change disease: prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day — remission in 90%.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Membranous nephropathy","detail":"Watchful waiting or immunosuppression (cyclophosphamide or rituximab) — depending on PLA2R antibodies and progression."},{"naam":"ACE inhibitor","detail":"Antiproteinuric effect + blood pressure control."},{"naam":"Anticoagulation","detail":"Consider with serum albumin <20 g/L or renal vein thrombosis."}]},{"_i":278,"q":"65-year-old man, kidney failure, diabetes for 15 years, retinopathy. Urinalysis: microalbuminuria (280 mg/g creatinine). eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m². Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Diabetic nephropathy","IgA nephropathy","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","FSGS"],"ex":"Diabetic nephropathy: microalbuminuria → macroalbuminuria → GFR decline. Concurrent retinopathy confirms microvascular involvement.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Diabetic nephropathy: most common cause of end-stage kidney disease (ESRD) worldwide. Stages: normoalbuminuria → microalbuminuria (30-300 mg/g) → macroalbuminuria (>300 mg/g) → GFR decline.","wiki_redflag":"Proteinuria in diabetes without retinopathy: consider alternative diagnosis (IgA, hypertensive nephrosclerosis) — biopsy if in doubt.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hyperglycemia cascade","desc":"Chronic hyperglycemia → advanced glycation end-products (AGE), PKC activation, TGF-β upregulation → mesangial expansion → Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (pathognomonic on biopsy) → glomerulosclerosis."},{"title":"RAAS overactivation","desc":"Increased intraglomerular pressure via efferent arteriole constriction → accelerates progression. ACE inhibitor/ARB lowers this pressure and proteinuria."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"IgA nephropathy","desc":"Hematuria + proteinuria, often after infection, IgA deposits on immunofluorescence."},{"label":"Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","desc":"Long-standing hypertension, mild proteinuria, no retinopathy."},{"label":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)","desc":"Severe proteinuria, biopsy needed for diagnosis — can be caused by obesity or HIV."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB): antiproteinuric + nephroprotective — first choice.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Glucose control","detail":"HbA1c <53 mmol/mol (7%)."},{"naam":"SGLT2 inhibitor","detail":"Nephroprotective regardless of blood glucose (CREDENCE, DAPA-CKD) — second choice alongside ACE inhibitor."},{"naam":"Finerenone","detail":"Non-steroidal mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (MRA) — additional nephroprotection in DM2 + CKD."}]},{"_i":279,"q":"45-year-old man, hypertension, hematuria, kidney pain. Ultrasound: bilaterally enlarged kidneys with multiple cysts. Father had the same. Diagnosis?","a":["Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)","Multiple renal carcinomas","Medullary sponge kidneys","Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome"],"ex":"ADPKD: bilaterally enlarged cystic kidneys + family history. PKD1 (85%) or PKD2 (15%) — most common hereditary kidney disease.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD): progressive cyst growth → compression of kidney parenchyma → kidney failure in 50% before age 60. Extrarenal: liver cysts (90%), intracranial aneurysms (10%), mitral valve regurgitation.","wiki_redflag":"Intracranial aneurysms in 10% of ADPKD patients — rupture = subarachnoid hemorrhage. Screening with cerebral MRA with positive family history of cerebral hemorrhage.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Pathogenesis","desc":"PKD1/PKD2 mutations disrupt ciliary signaling in tubular epithelium → mTOR overactivation → abnormal cell proliferation and cyst formation."},{"title":"Progression","desc":"Total kidney volume (TKV) on MRI is biomarker for progression rate — basis for tolvaptan indication."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ARPKD (autosomal recessive)","desc":"Presentation in children, liver fibrosis + portal hypertension."},{"label":"Acquired cystic kidney disease","desc":"After long-term dialysis — no family history."},{"label":"Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL)","desc":"Cysts + hemangioblastomas + renal cell carcinoma — VHL gene mutation."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Blood pressure control: ACE inhibitor (target <110/75 mmHg in younger patients).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Tolvaptan","detail":"Vasopressin-2 receptor antagonist — slows cyst growth in rapidly growing ADPKD (TKV >750 mL). Watch for liver toxicity."},{"naam":"Aneurysm screening","detail":"Cerebral MRA with family history of subarachnoid hemorrhage or symptoms (headache)."}]},{"_i":280,"q":"Patient with chronic kidney disease (eGFR 18 mL/min). Lab: K+ 6.2 mmol/L, pH 7.28, HCO3- 14 mmol/L, ECG: peaked T waves. Which treatment has the highest priority?","a":["Intravenous calcium gluconate","Potassium-restricted diet","Oral sodium bicarbonate","Intravenous furosemide"],"ex":"Hyperkalemia + ECG changes (peaked T waves) = life-threatening. First step: IV calcium gluconate for myocardial stabilization.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"ECG progression in hyperkalemia: peaked T waves → PR prolongation, wide QRS → sinusoidal pattern → ventricular fibrillation. Treatment in three steps: (1) myocardial stabilization, (2) K+ intracellular, (3) remove K+.","wiki_redflag":"K+ >6.5 mmol/L or sinusoidal ECG pattern = imminent ventricular fibrillation. Immediate IV calcium gluconate — do not wait for laboratory confirmation if ECG changes are present.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Myocardial stabilization","desc":"Calcium gluconate 10% 10 mL IV over 2-3 min — raises action potential threshold. Works within 5 min but effect lasts only 30-60 min."},{"title":"K+ intracellular","desc":"Insulin 10 U + glucose 50 mL 50% IV → Na/K-ATPase activation → K+ falls 0.5-1 mmol/L within 30 min. Duration 4-6 hours."},{"title":"Remove K+","desc":"Kayexalate or patiromer (oral), IV furosemide with residual diuresis, hemodialysis in dialysis-requiring renal insufficiency."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"K+ 5.5-6.0 without ECG changes","desc":"Diet (potassium restriction), medication review (stop ACE inhibitor, potassium-sparing diuretic), kayexalate."},{"label":"Pseudohyperkalemia","desc":"Hemolysis during blood draw — check with new sample before treatment."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"ECG changes: calcium gluconate 10% 10 mL IV over 2-3 min → repeat after 5 min if needed.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Drive K+ intracellularly","detail":"Insulin 10 U + glucose 50% 50 mL IV. Salbutamol 10-20 mg nebulized as adjunct."},{"naam":"Remove K+","detail":"Kayexalate or patiromer, IV furosemide with diuresis, hemodialysis in refractory hyperkalemia."},{"naam":"Monitoring","detail":"Continuous ECG + K+ check every hour until K+ <5.5 mmol/L."}]},{"_i":281,"q":"22-year-old woman, pain with urination, frequent urgency, cloudy urine, fever 37.8°C. Urinalysis: leukocyturia, nitrite positive. No flank pain. Diagnosis?","a":["Uncomplicated urinary tract infection (cystitis)","Pyelonephritis","Urethritis due to Chlamydia","Interstitial cystitis"],"ex":"Cystitis: dysuria + pollakiuria + nitrite positive — without fever or flank pain. Pathogen: E. coli (85%). Treatment: nitrofurantoin or trimethoprim.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Urinary tract infection (UTI): uncomplicated (women, no anatomical abnormality) vs. complicated (men, pregnancy, obstruction, catheter, diabetes). Pathogens: E. coli, Staphylococcus saprophyticus (young women).","wiki_redflag":"UTI in men or children always prompts more extensive investigation — anatomical abnormalities (hydronephrosis, vesicoureteral reflux) must be excluded.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Ascent","desc":"Bacteria colonize the periurethral region → ascend via the urethra to the bladder → cystitis. With further ascent to renal parenchyma → pyelonephritis."},{"title":"Nitrite test","desc":"Gram-negative bacteria reduce nitrate → nitrite. Combination nitrite + leukocyte esterase = high predictive value."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Pyelonephritis","desc":"Flank pain + fever >38°C + nausea — renal parenchyma involved, consider IV antibiotics."},{"label":"Urethritis (Chlamydia/gonorrhea)","desc":"No nitrite, atypical sediment, sexually active, cave notifiable disease."},{"label":"Interstitial cystitis","desc":"Chronic, sterile urine sediment — bladder biopsy diagnosis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Uncomplicated cystitis: nitrofurantoin 100 mg 2×/day for 5 days or trimethoprim 200 mg 2×/day for 7 days.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Resistance","detail":"Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin) not first choice — reserve for pyelonephritis and complicated UTI."},{"naam":"Recurrence (≥3/year)","detail":"Low-dose prophylaxis or post-coital prophylaxis. Cranberry limited evidence."}]},{"_i":282,"q":"8-year-old boy, 2 weeks after throat infection: hematuria, edema, hypertension, oliguria. Complement C3 decreased. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","IgA nephropathy","Minimal change disease","HUS (hemolytic uremic syndrome)"],"ex":"Post-streptococcal GN: 1-3 weeks after throat infection (S. pyogenes) + hematuria + hypertension + low C3. Self-limiting in children.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis: immune complex deposition (IgG + C3) → complement activation → diffuse proliferative GN. Biopsy: subepithelial deposits ('humps'). ASLO titer confirms recent streptococcal infection.","wiki_redflag":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN): acute kidney failure + hematuria + >50% crescents on biopsy → urgent immunosuppression (methylprednisolone pulses ± cyclophosphamide). Hours count — irreversible kidney failure if delayed.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Immune complex cascade","desc":"Anti-streptococcal antibodies cross-react with glomerular antigens → immune complexes → complement activation → ROS and prostaglandins → GFR decline."},{"title":"Distinction from IgA nephropathy","desc":"IgA nephropathy gives hematuria within 24-48 hours during infection (synpharyngitic); post-streptococcal GN after 1-3 weeks. C3 is low in post-strep GN, normal in IgA."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"IgA nephropathy","desc":"Hematuria during (not after) infection, C3 normal, IgA deposits on immunofluorescence."},{"label":"HUS","desc":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia + thrombocytopenia + AKI after E. coli O157:H7 infection."},{"label":"Membranoproliferative GN","desc":"Persistently low C3 — distinguish from post-streptococcal GN (C3 recovers within 6-8 weeks)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Supportive: fluid restriction, antihypertensives, diuretic. Antibiotics for active streptococcal infection.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Prognosis children","detail":"Complete recovery in >95% — C3 normalizes within 6-8 weeks."},{"naam":"Adults","detail":"Higher chance of chronic kidney disease — follow-up kidney function and blood pressure recommended."}]},{"_i":283,"q":"ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in all patients with chronic kidney disease because of the risk of further kidney damage.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE: ACE inhibitors are actually nephroprotective in chronic kidney disease — lower intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"ACE inhibitors (and angiotensin receptor blockers, ARBs): reduce efferent arteriole tone → intraglomerular pressure falls → less podocyte stress → less proteinuria → slowed progression of kidney disease.","wiki_redflag":"Bilateral renal artery stenosis: ACE inhibitor blocks efferent arteriole vasodilation → kidney perfusion pressure falls → acute ischemic kidney failure. Absolutely contraindicated.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hemodynamic effect at start","desc":"Creatinine rise <30% after starting ACE inhibitor is acceptable — hemodynamic adaptation, not true kidney damage."},{"title":"Stop criteria","desc":"Creatinine rise >30%, K+ >5.5 mmol/L or bilateral renal artery stenosis — stop immediately."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Absolutely contraindicated","desc":"Bilateral renal artery stenosis (ischemic kidney failure), pregnancy (teratogenic — ACE inhibitor embryopathy)."},{"label":"Caution with eGFR <30","desc":"Lower benefit-risk ratio, but not absolutely contraindicated — intensive monitoring required."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start ACE inhibitor in CKD with proteinuria >300 mg/day — unless contraindication.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Monitoring","detail":"Creatinine and K+ after 1-2 weeks — creatinine rise <30% acceptable, K+ <5.5 mmol/L."},{"naam":"Targets","detail":"Blood pressure <130/80 mmHg, proteinuria as low as possible (<300 mg/24 h if achievable)."}]},{"_i":284,"q":"A patient with an eGFR of 15 mL/min needs hemodialysis by definition.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE: dialysis decision based on uremic symptoms — not on eGFR alone. Some patients with eGFR <15 are minimally symptomatic.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Renal replacement therapy (RRT): hemodialysis (HD), peritoneal dialysis (PD) or kidney transplantation. ESRD = eGFR <15 by definition — but this is not the threshold for dialysis.","wiki_redflag":"Uremic pericarditis and uremic encephalopathy are absolute indications for acute dialysis — delay increases risk of pericardial tamponade and coma.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Preparation at eGFR 10-15","desc":"Create AV fistula (at least 6 months before HD start) or evaluation for kidney transplantation — plan early."},{"title":"Dialysis indications","desc":"Uremic symptoms (pruritus, pericarditis, encephalopathy), fluid overload refractory to diuretics, refractory hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis (pH <7.1)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Kidney transplantation","desc":"Superior to dialysis in survival and quality of life — always first choice in suitable candidate."},{"label":"Pre-emptive transplantation","desc":"Before starting dialysis with suitable living donor — optimal result."},{"label":"Peritoneal dialysis (CAPD)","desc":"At home, continuous treatment — suitable for independent patients, less cardiovascular burden than HD."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start RRT with uremic symptoms or refractory complications — not purely on eGFR threshold.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Preparation (eGFR 10-15)","detail":"AV fistula (at least 6 months before HD) or start transplantation evaluation."},{"naam":"Modality choice","detail":"HD 3× per week in center or CAPD at home — decision with patient based on preference, comorbidity and residual kidney function."}]},{"_i":285,"q":"Hyponatremia (Na+ <135 mmol/L) is always a sign of a sodium deficit in the body.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE: hyponatremia is a concentration disorder — says nothing about total sodium amount. Cause determines treatment (SIADH, heart failure, volume depletion).","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Hyponatremia (Na+ <135): (1) Hypervolemic — heart failure, liver cirrhosis, nephrotic. (2) Euvolemic — SIADH, hypothyroidism, adrenocortical insufficiency. (3) Hypovolemic — vomiting, diarrhea, diuretics.","wiki_redflag":"Too rapid correction of hyponatremia (>10-12 mmol/L per 24 h): osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis) → dysarthria, dysphagia, paraplegia — irreversible.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"SIADH","desc":"Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH: ADH elevated despite low osmolality → water retention → hyponatremia. Causes: lung carcinoma (paraneoplastic), meningitis, medication (carbamazepine, thiazides, SSRI)."},{"title":"SIADH diagnosis","desc":"Urine osmolality >100 mOsm/kg + urine sodium >30 mmol/L in hyponatremia with euvolemia."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Pseudohyponatremia","desc":"With hypertriglyceridemia or hyperproteinemia — laboratory artifact, no true hyponatremia."},{"label":"SIADH","desc":"Euvolemic, urine Na high, osmolality high — treatment: fluid restriction."},{"label":"Heart failure/cirrhosis","desc":"Hypervolemic, edema present — treatment: fluid restriction + diuretics, underlying cause."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Correction NEVER faster than 8-10 mmol/L per 24 hours — use hypertonic NaCl 3% only with severe symptoms (seizure, coma).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Hypovolemic","detail":"NaCl 0.9% IV — volume restoration lowers ADH → Na+ corrects spontaneously."},{"naam":"SIADH","detail":"Fluid restriction 500-1000 mL/day. Tolvaptan (V2 antagonist) in refractory SIADH."},{"naam":"Hypervolemic","detail":"Fluid restriction + furosemide. Treat underlying heart failure or cirrhosis."}]},{"_i":286,"q":"Which urinalysis feature distinguishes nephritic from nephrotic syndrome?","a":["Red blood cell casts in the urine sediment","Proteinuria >3.5 g/24 h","Edema and hypoalbuminemia","Hypercholesterolemia"],"ex":"RBC casts in urine sediment = pathognomonic for nephritic syndrome (glomerular hematuria).","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Urine sediment: RBC casts = glomerulonephritis (nephritic). Granular casts ('muddy brown') = acute tubular necrosis (ATN). Fatty casts + oval fat bodies = nephrotic syndrome.","wiki_redflag":"Crescentic glomerulonephritis (RPGN): acute kidney failure + hematuria + RBC casts + >50% crescents on biopsy → urgent immunosuppression (methylprednisolone pulses). Hours count — irreversible kidney failure if delayed.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Nephritic syndrome","desc":"Glomerular hematuria → dysmorphic erythrocytes (acanthocytes) + RBC casts + moderate proteinuria + hypertension + oliguria + AKI."},{"title":"Nephrotic syndrome","desc":"Podocyte dysfunction → selective protein loss → hypoalbuminemia → edema, hyperlipidemia, thrombosis tendency. Sediment normal or fatty casts."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"IgA nephropathy","desc":"Nephritic + RBC casts, recurrent episodes after upper respiratory tract infection."},{"label":"Minimal change disease (MCNS)","desc":"Nephrotic — urine sediment normal (no casts)."},{"label":"Lupus nephritis","desc":"Can give both nephritic and nephrotic picture — 'full house' immunofluorescence."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Nephritic syndrome with AKI or oliguria: immediate nephrology consult for biopsy and immunosuppression.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Minimal change disease","detail":"Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day — remission in >80% within 4 weeks."},{"naam":"IgA nephropathy","detail":"ACE inhibitor + SGLT2 inhibitor. With progression: methylprednisolone course."},{"naam":"Lupus nephritis class III/IV","detail":"Mycophenolate mofetil + high-dose corticosteroids."}]},{"_i":287,"q":"55-year-old man, recurrent kidney stones. CT shows radiopaque stones (calcium oxalate). 24-hour urine test: hypercalciuria. Which disorder must be excluded as underlying cause?","a":["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Gout (hyperuricemia)","Cystinuria","Struvite infection stones"],"ex":"Hypercalciuria + calcium oxalate stones: always exclude primary hyperparathyroidism (elevated parathyroid hormone) via PTH measurement — treatable and must not be missed.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Kidney stone types: calcium oxalate/phosphate (70%, radiopaque), uric acid stones (15%, radiolucent), struvite/infection stones (10%, radiopaque, staghorn-shaped), cystine (1%, hereditary). Non-contrast CT abdomen: gold standard.","wiki_redflag":"Fever + obstructive ureter/kidney stone = infected obstruction with urosepsis risk → emergency surgery (drainage via double-J stent or nephrostomy) + IV antibiotics.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Calcium oxalate stones","desc":"Hypercalciuria (idiopathic, primary hyperparathyroidism (HPT), immobilization), hyperoxaluria (Crohn, malabsorption), low citrate excretion (metabolic acidosis)."},{"title":"Uric acid stones","desc":"Hyperuricemia + acidic urine (pH <5.5) — treatable with urine alkalinization and allopurinol."},{"title":"Struvite infection stones","desc":"Urease-producing bacteria (Proteus mirabilis, Klebsiella) — staghorn-shaped, fills renal pelvis."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Uric acid stones","desc":"treatable with urine alkalinization (sodium bicarbonate, allopurinol)."},{"label":"Struvite","desc":"antibiotics + surgical removal."},{"label":"Cystine","desc":"D-penicillamine, generous fluid intake."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute: analgesia (NSAID or morphine), hydration, alpha-blocker (tamsulosin) to promote passage.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Elective","detail":"extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (ESWL) or ureteroscopy."},{"naam":"Prevention","detail":">2 L diuresis/day."}]},{"_i":288,"q":"35-year-old woman, recurrent urinary tract infections after menopause, now also flank pain and fever 39°C. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Acute pyelonephritis","Uncomplicated cystitis","Nephrolithiasis","Renal abscess"],"ex":"Pyelonephritis = cystitis symptoms + flank pain (costovertebral angle tenderness) + fever ≥38°C. Treatment: ciprofloxacin 7 days or IV in severe course.","dl":"Nephrology","wiki_kern":"Pyelonephritis: ascent of E. coli (80%), Klebsiella or other gram-negative bacteria to the renal parenchyma. Diagnosis: clinical picture + leukocyturia + nitrite + urine culture.","wiki_redflag":"Obstruction + pyelonephritis = urosepsis — urgent imaging (ultrasound/CT), drainage (double-J stent or nephrostomy) and IV antibiotics are urgent. Urosepsis mortality 20-40%.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Urosepsis mechanism","desc":"Obstruction impairs drainage pathway → bacteria and lipopolysaccharide (LPS) enter bloodstream → cytokine storm → SIRS → organ dysfunction."},{"title":"ESBL problem","desc":"ESBL-producing E. coli increasing — with risk factors (travel, hospital admission) consider meropenem or IV fosfomycin."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Nephrolithiasis","desc":"colicky pain (not percussion-sensitive or sometimes so), no fever initially."},{"label":"Renal abscess","desc":"persistent fever after antibiotics, CT diagnosis, drainage needed."},{"label":"Cystitis","desc":"no flank pain or fever."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Outpatient pyelonephritis: ciprofloxacin 500 mg 2× daily for 7 days (or co-trimoxazole if sensitive).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Hospital admission if","detail":"vomiting (oral therapy not possible), sepsis, pregnancy, obstruction, immunosuppression."},{"naam":"IV antibiotics","detail":"Ceftriaxone 2 g 1× daily or piperacillin-tazobactam with ESBL risk; switch to oral after clinical improvement."},{"naam":"Imaging","detail":"Ultrasound/CT with persistent fever after 72 h: exclude obstruction or abscess."}]},{"_i":289,"q":"32-year-old woman, depressed for 3 weeks, sleep problems, weight loss, concentration problems, guilt feelings, no suicidal thoughts. DSM-5 diagnosis?","a":["Major depressive disorder","Dysthymia (persistent depressive disorder)","Bipolar disorder type II","Grief reaction"],"ex":"MDD: ≥5 of the 9 DSM-5 criteria for at least 2 weeks, including depressed mood or anhedonia. Dysthymia: milder but chronic depression (≥2 years). Bipolar type II: depressive episodes + hypomanic (not manic) episodes.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"DSM-5 criteria MDD (≥5 present ≥2 weeks): depressed mood, anhedonia, weight change, sleep disturbance, psychomotor agitation/retardation, fatigue, feelings of worthlessness/guilt, concentration problems, suicidal thoughts. At least depressed mood OR anhedonia.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Monoamine deficiency hypothesis","desc":"reduced serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"SSRIs (selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors) are first choice: fluoxetine, sertraline, paroxetine."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Effect after 4-6 weeks."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"Combination with cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) is superior."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Bipolar","desc":"manic or hypomanic episode ≥1× previously."},{"label":"Grief reaction","desc":"normal after loss, less severe, no guilt feelings about self."},{"label":"Hypothyroidism","desc":"also depressive features, determine TSH in every new depression."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Mild-moderate: CBT or SSRI (equivalent).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Severe","detail":"SSRI + CBT or pharmacotherapy + psychiatric consultation."},{"naam":"Treatment duration","detail":"≥6 months after remission (recurrence prevention)."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"ECT in treatment-resistant or severe depression."}]},{"_i":290,"q":"25-year-old man, past week grandiose self-esteem, barely sleeping but full of energy, excessive talking, flight of ideas, impulsive spending. No drugs. Diagnosis?","a":["Manic episode (bipolar disorder type I)","Hypomanic episode (bipolar disorder type II)","Schizophrenia","Borderline personality disorder"],"ex":"Manic episode: elevated mood + ≥3 B criteria (grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, flight of ideas, pressured speech, goal-directed behavior, impulsivity) ≥7 days or hospitalization required. Hypomanic: same but ≤4 days and no admission/severe consequences.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Bipolar disorder type I: at least one manic episode (with or without depression). Type II: hypomanic episodes + depressive episodes (never manic).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Lithium is cornerstone of maintenance treatment: mood stabilizer."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Works via inhibition of inositol monophosphatase and GSK-3β."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Narrow therapeutic window (0.6-1.2 mEq/L)."},{"title":"Alternatives","desc":"valproate, lamotrigine (depression prevention), quetiapine."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Borderline","desc":"unstable moods but short-lived (hours), related to interpersonal stress."},{"label":"Schizophrenia","desc":"delusions/hallucinations predominant, no manic mood elevation."},{"label":"Drug intoxication (cocaine, amphetamine)","desc":"history."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute mania: antipsychotic (olanzapine, risperidone, quetiapine) + mood stabilizer.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Lithium","detail":"maintenance."},{"naam":"Valproate","detail":"rapid choice in acute mania."},{"naam":"ECT","detail":"in severe treatment-resistant mania."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Psychoeducation about medication nonadherence (major risk)."}]},{"_i":291,"q":"28-year-old woman, recurrent, unexpected panic attacks with palpitations, sweating, shortness of breath, fear of dying. She now avoids public places. Diagnosis?","a":["Panic disorder with agoraphobia","Social anxiety disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder","Specific phobia"],"ex":"Panic disorder: recurrent unexpected panic attacks + persistent concern about new attacks or behavioral change. Agoraphobia: avoidance of situations from which escape would be difficult. The combination is frequent.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Panic attack: sudden intense fear with ≥4 physical/cognitive symptoms (palpitations, sweating, trembling, dyspnea, chest pain, nausea, dizziness, derealization, fear of losing control/dying). Peaks within 10 minutes.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Neurobiology: amygdala hypersensitivity, locus coeruleus hyperactivity (norepinephrine), overreaction of the fight-or-flight system. Hyperventilation."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"CO2 decrease."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"respiratory alkalosis."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"paresthesias."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD)","desc":"chronic, diffuse, difficult-to-control worry (not episodic)."},{"label":"Social anxiety","desc":"fear of social evaluation."},{"label":"PTSD","desc":"anxiety after trauma."},{"label":"Exclude somatic disorders","desc":"pheochromocytoma, hyperthyroidism, arrhythmia."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"CBT (exposure techniques): proven most effective.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"SSRI (sertraline, paroxetine)","detail":"pharmacological first choice."},{"naam":"Benzodiazepines","detail":"short-term for acute attacks, not as maintenance treatment (dependence risk)."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Combination CBT + SSRI superior."}]},{"_i":292,"q":"70-year-old man, rapid onset over several days: disorientation in time and place, fluctuating consciousness, hallucinations (sees spiders), agitation at night. Was still lucid last week. Diagnosis?","a":["Delirium","Dementia","Psychosis","Alcohol withdrawal"],"ex":"Delirium: acutely (<48 h) developing disturbance of consciousness with attention problems, disorientation and fluctuating course. Characteristic: acute onset + fluctuating course + attention disturbance + organic cause. Dementia: chronic, progressive, no acute onset.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Delirium (CAM criteria): acute onset + fluctuating course + attention disturbance + disorganized thinking or disturbance of consciousness. Most common psychiatric disorder in hospital (15-30% of admissions).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Neurobiology","desc":"cholinergic deficit (acetylcholinesterase inhibitors can help), dopamine excess, GABA imbalance in alcohol withdrawal."},{"title":"Predisposing factors","desc":"age, dementia, polypharmacy."},{"title":"Precipitating factors","desc":"infection (UTI, pneumonia), medication (anticholinergics, opioids, benzodiazepines), dehydration, pain, retention."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Dementia","desc":"chronic, no acute onset, consciousness initially normal."},{"label":"Delirium superimposed on dementia","desc":"mixed and frequent."},{"label":"Psychosis (schizophrenia)","desc":"chronic, consciousness not impaired."},{"label":"Alcohol withdrawal","desc":"history, tremor, delirium tremens after 48-72 h abstinence."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Treat underlying cause.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Non-pharmacological","detail":"orientation, normalize sleep cycle, mobilization, no unnecessary catheters."},{"naam":"Pharmacological in severe agitation","detail":"low-dose haloperidol."},{"naam":"Alcohol withdrawal","detail":"benzodiazepines."}]},{"_i":293,"q":"22-year-old man, social withdrawal, bizarre belief that neighbors are spying on him through the TV, hearing voices commenting, flat affect. Symptoms >6 months. Diagnosis?","a":["Schizophrenia","Schizoaffective disorder","Bipolar disorder with psychotic features","Delusional disorder"],"ex":"Schizophrenia DSM-5: ≥2 core symptoms ≥1 month (delusions, hallucinations, disorganization, negative symptoms, catatonia) + social/occupational dysfunction + duration ≥6 months. Schizoaffective disorder: psychosis + prominent mood episodes simultaneously.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations): overactivity of dopaminergic system (mesolimbic pathway). Negative symptoms (flat affect, alogia, avolition, anhedonia): hypoactivity of prefrontal cortex.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Antipsychotics","desc":"blockade of D2 receptors (mesolimbic/mesocortical)."},{"title":"Typical (haloperidol)","desc":"effective but extrapyramidal adverse effects (parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia)."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Atypical (clozapine, quetiapine, risperidone): fewer EPS, more metabolic adverse effects."},{"title":"Clozapine","desc":"most effective in treatment resistance but agranulocytosis risk."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Schizoaffective disorder","desc":"psychotic symptoms also outside mood episodes."},{"label":"Bipolar with psychosis","desc":"psychosis only during mood episode."},{"label":"Delusional disorder","desc":"isolated non-bizarre delusion, no hallucinations or disorganization."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Antipsychotic (atypical first choice).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Psychosocial support","detail":"psychoeducation, work integration, family intervention."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Treatment ≥2 years after first episode."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Clozapine in treatment resistance (2 antipsychotics insufficient)."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Psychosocial rehabilitation."}]},{"_i":294,"q":"45-year-old woman, recurrent intrusive thoughts about contamination that she cannot stop. She washes her hands >50× per day to reduce anxiety, which helps temporarily. Diagnosis?","a":["Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)","Generalized anxiety disorder","Somatic symptom disorder","Specific phobia"],"ex":"OCD: obsessions (intrusive, egodystonic thoughts → anxiety) + compulsions (rituals to reduce anxiety). The compulsions provide temporary relief but reinforce obsessions in the long term. Egodystonic = the patient experiences the thoughts as strange/unwanted.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"OCD prevalence: 2-3%. Neurobiology: hyperactivity of orbitofrontal cortex + caudate nucleus.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Serotonin deficiency hypothesis","desc":"SSRI in higher dose than depression (e.g. fluoxetine 60-80 mg)."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"CBT with exposure and response prevention (ERP): first-choice treatment."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Neurosurgery (DBS) in severe treatment resistance."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"GAD","desc":"worry about real problems (work, health), not egodystonic."},{"label":"Irritable bowel syndrome-OCD overlap","desc":"somatic focus."},{"label":"ADHD","desc":"impulsive behavior but no rituals."},{"label":"Psychosis","desc":"delusions but not experienced as egodystonic."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"First choice: CBT-ERP (highest remission).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Pharmacological","detail":"SSRI (higher dose than depression, 12+ weeks)."},{"naam":"Clomipramine (TCA)","detail":"effective but more adverse effects."},{"naam":"Combination CBT + SSRI","detail":"superior in moderate/severe OCD."}]},{"_i":295,"q":"28-year-old soldier, returned from war zone 6 months ago. Re-experiencing, nightmares, avoids stimuli that remind him of trauma, hypervigilance, emotional numbness. Diagnosis?","a":["Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)","Acute stress disorder","Adjustment disorder","Major depressive disorder"],"ex":"PTSD: after exposure to traumatic event → ≥1 month: re-experiencing symptoms (flashbacks, nightmares) + avoidance + negative cognitive/mood symptoms + hyperarousal. Acute stress disorder: <1 month after trauma.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"PTSD: lifetime prevalence 8% in general population, 20-30% in war veterans. Neurobiology: amygdala hyperactivity, prefrontal cortex hypoactivity (extinction learning disturbed), hippocampal atrophy (negative memory).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Traumatic memory is not integrated → intrusions."},{"title":"Treatment","desc":"trauma-focused CBT (EMDR, prolonged exposure)."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"EMDR (eye movement desensitization and reprocessing): proven effective."},{"title":"SSRI (paroxetine, sertraline)","desc":"pharmacological first choice."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Adjustment disorder","desc":"less severe trauma, short-lived (<6 months), no re-experiencing."},{"label":"Depression","desc":"can be comorbid."},{"label":"Borderline","desc":"chronic instability, interpersonal pattern."},{"label":"Acute stress disorder","desc":"<1 month, sometimes dissociative."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR: first choice.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"SSRI","detail":"paroxetine or sertraline."},{"naam":"Prazosin (alpha-blocker)","detail":"effective for nightmares."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"No benzodiazepines (increase avoidance)."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Group therapy and peer support as adjunct."}]},{"_i":296,"q":"In acute psychosis, it is always safe to start with a high dose of antipsychotics to control symptoms faster.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. High doses of antipsychotics give more adverse effects (extrapyramidal symptoms, sedation, orthostatic hypotension) without faster efficacy. Start dose low, titrate guided by effect and tolerability. The antipsychotic effect only fully occurs after 2-6 weeks.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Antipsychotics: D2 receptor blockade in the mesolimbic system. Parkinsonian symptoms: D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal system.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Neuroleptic equivalents","desc":"all agents are expressed as chlorpromazine equivalents."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Atypical antipsychotics (quetiapine, olanzapine, clozapine): also serotonin antagonism → fewer EPS, more metabolic adverse effects (weight gain, diabetes)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Option 1","desc":"Serotonin syndrome vs. neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS): NMS = dopamine antagonist → high fever + rigidity + autonomic instability + decreased consciousness."},{"label":"Treatment NMS","desc":"stop antipsychotic, dantrolene, bromocriptine."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":297,"q":"Lithium is safe to use in patients with reduced kidney function without dose adjustment.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Lithium is exclusively renally excreted and has a narrow therapeutic window (0.6-1.2 mEq/L). In renal impairment, lithium accumulates → toxicity (tremor, cognitive disorders, seizures, renal damage). Always dose adjustment and close level monitoring.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Lithium toxicity: levels >1.5 mEq/L → nausea, diarrhea, coarse tremor. >2.0: lethargy, confusion, ataxia. >3.0: seizures, coma, irreversible renal/neurological damage. Dehydration (also due to NSAID, thiazide) increases lithium level → always warn.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Lithium and sodium are competitively reabsorbed in the proximal tubule. With sodium deficiency (dehydration, low salt intake)."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"more lithium reabsorption."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"level rises. Check quarterly: lithium level, kidney function, TSH (lithium causes hypothyroidism with long-term use)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Valproate","desc":"renal clearance less dependent, but monitor liver function."},{"label":"Lamotrigine","desc":"particularly safe in kidney disease, no level monitoring required."},{"label":"Carbamazepine","desc":"also renal excretion, but less narrow window."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":298,"q":"Suicidality in a depressed patient is an absolute contraindication to SSRI treatment.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. SSRIs are first choice in depression, also with suicidality. In the first weeks, activation can occur (more energy while mood is still low) → slightly increased short-term risk in first 2 weeks → close follow-up. Not treating depression is a greater risk.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"FDA black box warning: increased suicide risk in young people <25 years in the first weeks of SSRI treatment. In adults and older adults: SSRI lowers suicide risk long term.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Activation effect","desc":"energy level recovers faster than mood → "dangerous window"."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Benzodiazepines as short-term bridge in severe agitation/anxiety."},{"title":"Always safety evaluation","desc":"intent, plan, means, protective factors."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Suicide risk factors","desc":"previous attempt, male sex, single, access to means, substance abuse, chronic pain."},{"label":"Protective factors","desc":"social network, children, faith community, willingness to receive treatment."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":299,"q":"Which feature distinguishes a depressive episode in bipolar disorder from unipolar depression in treatment choice?","a":["SSRI monotherapy can trigger a manic episode in bipolar depression","Bipolar depression responds faster to CBT","Bipolar depression always has suicidality","In unipolar depression, lithium contraindications are higher"],"ex":"SSRI monotherapy in bipolar depression can trigger a manic switch or cycle acceleration. Therefore, in bipolar depression, a mood stabilizer (lithium, quetiapine, lamotrigine) is given as the foundation, and SSRIs only cautiously and always in combination with a stabilizer.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Bipolar depression treatment differs fundamentally from unipolar: (1) Always mood stabilizer as foundation. (2) SSRI only in combination, not as monotherapy. (3) Quetiapine is proven effective for bipolar depression and has FDA approval.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Manic switch","desc":"bipolar patient becomes acutely manic due to antidepressant."},{"title":"Risk","desc":"higher with TCA (tricyclic) than with SSRI, lowest with lamotrigine."},{"title":"Lamotrigine","desc":"proven for bipolar depression prevention, not for acute mania."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Unipolar depression","desc":"SSRI monotherapy or CBT monotherapy are first choice."},{"label":"Option 2","desc":"Bipolar I vs."},{"label":"II","desc":"both risky with SSRI monotherapy."},{"label":"Postpartum psychosis","desc":"more often associated with bipolar disorder."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":300,"q":"16-year-old young person, extremely little eating (BMI 15.5), disturbed body image ("I am too fat"), amenorrhea, denial of illness. Diagnosis?","a":["Anorexia nervosa (restricting type)","Bulimia nervosa","Binge-eating disorder","Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID)"],"ex":"Anorexia nervosa: BMI <17.5 + intense fear of weight gain + disturbed body image + denial of seriousness. Most common in adolescent women. Highest mortality of all psychiatric disorders (death from medical complications + suicide).","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Medical complications anorexia: bradycardia, hypotension, hyponatremia, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis (with purging behavior), osteoporosis, lanugo, edema during refeeding. Refeeding syndrome: danger with rapid refeeding (phosphate, K+, Mg falls).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Restricting type","desc":"calorie restriction only."},{"title":"Binge-purge type","desc":"also binge eating + compensatory behavior (vomiting, laxatives)."},{"title":"Treatment","desc":"multidisciplinary (psychiatry, dietetics, somatics)."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"FBT (family-based treatment) most effective in adolescents."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Bulimia","desc":"normal weight, binge eating + compensation, no disturbed body schema as in AN."},{"label":"Binge-eating","desc":"binge eating without compensation, overweight."},{"label":"ARFID","desc":"no weight phobia, selective eating due to sensory aversion."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Outpatient if possible.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Admission with BMI <13 or medical complications."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Refeed slowly (refeeding syndrome)."},{"naam":"Psychotherapy","detail":"CBT (effective in bulimia), FBT (adolescents)."},{"naam":"Pharmacological","detail":"no proven medication for AN; fluoxetine for bulimia (FDA-approved)."}]},{"_i":301,"q":"40-year-old man, for many years deep distrust of others, interprets neutral comments as threatening, holds grudges, attributes bad intentions to others. No psychosis. Diagnosis?","a":["Paranoid personality disorder","Schizophrenia paranoid type","Schizoid personality disorder","Delusional disorder"],"ex":"Paranoid personality disorder (cluster A): pervasive distrust and suspicion, assumes that others want to harm him, but no psychosis (delusions, hallucinations). Chronic, ego-syntonic (the patient experiences his thinking as normal).","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Personality disorders: cluster A (schizoid, schizotypal, paranoid), cluster B (antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic), cluster C (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive). Characteristics: pervasive, inflexible, egosyntonic, early onset.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Personality disorders are difficult to treat: ego-syntonic → limited treatment motivation."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Psychotherapy (dialectical behavior therapy in borderline) is more effective than pharmacotherapy."},{"title":"Medication","desc":"symptomatic (antipsychotics for paranoia, SSRI for impulsivity)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Schizophrenia","desc":"psychotic symptoms (true delusions, hallucinations)."},{"label":"Delusional disorder","desc":"isolated delusion, otherwise intact functioning."},{"label":"Schizoid","desc":"socially withdrawn but no suspicion."},{"label":"Schizotypal","desc":"magical thinking, odd behavior."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Psychotherapy: cognitive therapy helps with paranoid thinking.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Therapeutic relationship is difficult due to distrust."},{"naam":"Pharmacotherapy","detail":"no proven effect, sometimes low-dose antipsychotic in severe symptoms."}]},{"_i":302,"q":"38-year-old woman, unstable relationships, chronic emptiness, impulsivity (self-harm, risky behavior), intense mood swings related to interpersonal stressors. Diagnosis?","a":["Borderline personality disorder","Bipolar disorder type II","Cyclothymia","Major depressive disorder with impulsivity"],"ex":"Borderline PD (cluster B): unstable relationships + self-image + affect + impulsivity. Mood swings last hours (not days as in bipolar) and are interpersonally triggered. Self-harm and suicide attempts are frequent but mostly parasuicidal.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"BPD neurobiology: increased amygdala reactivity + prefrontal hypoactivity → emotion dysregulation. High comorbidity with depression, PTSD, substance use disorders.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"DBT (dialectical behavior therapy, Linehan): gold standard treatment for BPD."},{"title":"Modules","desc":"mindfulness, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness, distress tolerance."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"MBT (mentalization-based treatment): alternative."},{"title":"Pharmacotherapy","desc":"symptomatic."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Bipolar disorder","desc":"mood swings last days to weeks, autonomous (not interpersonally triggered), sleep need changed."},{"label":"BPD","desc":"hours, interpersonal, sleep cycle relatively intact."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"DBT outpatient (2 years).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Systemic therapy as adjunct."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Avoid hospitalization unless acute safety."},{"naam":"Pharmacological","detail":"low-dose antipsychotic for dissociation/paranoia, SSRI for depression/impulsivity."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Mood stabilizer for affective instability."}]},{"_i":303,"q":"60-year-old man, progressive memory decline over 3 years, forgets appointments, gets lost at home, apraxia, reduced judgment. MRI: bitemporal/parietal atrophy. Diagnosis?","a":["Alzheimer's disease","Vascular dementia","Lewy body dementia","Frontotemporal dementia"],"ex":"Alzheimer: most common dementia (60-70%). Insidious onset, progressive, dominant anterograde memory loss, bitemporal/parietal atrophy on MRI. Lewy body: also parkinsonism + fluctuating cognition + visual hallucinations.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Dementia classification: Alzheimer (60%), vascular (15%), Lewy body (15%), FTD (5-10%), mixed. Alzheimer biomarkers: amyloid-β (reduced in CSF, positive on PET), tau and phospho-tau (elevated in CSF).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Amyloid cascade hypothesis: APP."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"amyloid-β aggregation."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"senile plaques."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"tau tangle formation."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"neuronal loss. Cholinergic deficit."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Vascular dementia","desc":"stepwise course, cerebrovascular pathology on MRI (lacunar infarcts), executive dysfunction."},{"label":"FTD","desc":"personality and behavioral change, language disorder, frontal atrophy."},{"label":"Lewy body","desc":"REM sleep behavior disorder, parkinsonism, DLB triad."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors: donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Memantine (NMDA antagonist)","detail":"in moderate/severe Alzheimer."},{"naam":"New agents","detail":"lecanemab (anti-amyloid mAb) slows progression (FDA 2023)."},{"naam":"Non-pharmacological","detail":"daily structure, environmental adaptation."}]},{"_i":304,"q":"Which laboratory test is always indicated in a new depressive episode to exclude a somatic cause?","a":["TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone)","Morning cortisol","Prolactin","Lithium level"],"ex":"TSH is standard in every new depression: hypothyroidism often presents with depressive symptoms and is easily treatable. Other basics: complete blood count, electrolytes, glucose, kidney function, liver enzymes. Cortisol if Cushing/Addison suspected, prolactin with galactorrhea.","dl":"Psychiatry","wiki_kern":"Somatic screening in psychiatric presentations: TSH (hypothyroidism/hyperthyroidism), blood glucose (hypoglycemia → anxiety/delirium), Ca2+ (hypercalcemia → psychosis/depression), vitamin B12/folate (deficiency → depression/dementia), CRP/ESR (inflammation), alcohol/drug screen.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hypothyroidism mimic","desc":"fatigue, weight gain, depression, cognitive slowing, cold intolerance."},{"title":"Hyperthyroidism","desc":"anxiety, agitation, insomnia."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Both can mimic psychiatric diagnosis."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"TSH has the highest sensitivity."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"In psychosis or delirium","desc":"also blood gas (CO2 retention), ammonia (hepatic encephalopathy), brain MRI (structural), LP (meningitis/encephalitis)."},{"label":"In first psychosis <25 years","desc":"extensive somatic screening mandatory."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":305,"q":"7-year-old child, itchy red spots with blisters, first head then trunk and extremities, fever 38°C. Household contact ill last week. Diagnosis?","a":["Varicella (chickenpox)","Hand-foot-mouth disease","Measles","Scarlet fever"],"ex":"Polymorphic exanthem in all stages simultaneously = characteristic of chickenpox (varicella-zoster virus (VZV) primary infection).","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Varicella-zoster virus (VZV): primary infection = chickenpox. Reactivation later in life = shingles (herpes zoster).","wiki_redflag":"Immunocompromised patients, pregnant women and neonates: severe course possible — start acyclovir immediately.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Polymorphic exanthem","desc":"Lesions in all stages (macule → papule → vesicle → crust) simultaneously present — distinguishing feature."},{"title":"Mucosal involvement","desc":"Vesicles also possible in oral cavity."},{"title":"Contagiousness","desc":"Respiratory and contact transmission. Contagious from 1-2 days before rash until all lesions are crusted."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hand-foot-mouth disease (Coxsackievirus)","desc":"Vesicles on palms + soles + mouth, milder fever."},{"label":"Measles","desc":"High fever, Koplik spots, morbilliform rash — no vesicles."},{"label":"Scarlet fever","desc":"Streptococcal toxin, fine punctate exanthem, 'strawberry tongue'."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Healthy child: symptomatic (antihistamines, paracetamol). Avoid aspirin (Reye syndrome).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Acyclovir","detail":"Immunosuppressed, pregnant women, neonates, adults — start as early as possible."},{"naam":"Vaccination","detail":"Live attenuated vaccine (Varivax) — 2 doses."}]},{"_i":306,"q":"55-year-old man, years of silvery scaly plaques on knees, elbows and scalp, sharply demarcated. Nail abnormalities (pitting). Diagnosis?","a":["Psoriasis vulgaris","Seborrheic dermatitis","Lichen planus","Nummular eczema"],"ex":"Psoriasis: sharply demarcated silvery scaly plaques on extensor surfaces (knees/elbows/scalp). Nail abnormalities (pitting, onycholysis) in 50%.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Psoriasis: T-cell-mediated autoimmune disease with accelerated keratinocyte proliferation (turnover 3-4 days vs. normally 28). Prevalence 2-3%, genetic component (HLA-Cw6).","wiki_redflag":"Erythrodermic psoriasis (>90% body surface area): risk of hypothermia, heart failure and infection — emergency admission.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Inflammation via Th17","desc":"IL-17, IL-23 and TNF-α activate keratinocytes → accelerated cell division → silvery white scales."},{"title":"Koebner phenomenon","desc":"New lesions arise at sites of skin trauma (scratch, surgical scar)."},{"title":"Triggers","desc":"Infection (streptococci), stress, alcohol, medication (lithium, beta-blockers, NSAIDs)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Seborrheic dermatitis","desc":"Also scalp, but greasy yellow scaling, less sharply demarcated, not on extensors."},{"label":"Eczema","desc":"Itching predominant, flexural location, less sharp demarcation."},{"label":"Lichen planus","desc":"Purple polygonal papules, Wickham striae, preference for wrists — can resemble psoriasis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Topical: corticosteroids + vitamin D analogues (calcipotriol).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Moderate","detail":"UVB phototherapy, tar preparations (scalp)."},{"naam":"Severe","detail":"Methotrexate (MTX), ciclosporin, acitretin."},{"naam":"Biologics","detail":"Anti-TNF (adalimumab), anti-IL-17 (secukinumab), anti-IL-23 (guselkumab) with insufficient response."}]},{"_i":307,"q":"4-year-old child, itchy eczema in popliteal fossae and elbow creases, dry skin, seasonal worsening. Mother has hay fever. Diagnosis?","a":["Atopic dermatitis","Allergic contact dermatitis","Inverse psoriasis","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"ex":"Atopic eczema: itching + flexural location (popliteal fossae/elbow creases) + dry skin. Familial atopy (eczema + asthma + rhinitis = atopic triad).","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Atopic dermatitis: filaggrin mutations → skin barrier defect → dry skin and allergen penetration → Th2-mediated inflammation (IL-4, IL-13) with IgE overproduction.","wiki_redflag":"Eczema herpeticum: herpes simplex superinfection on infected eczema — generalized painful vesicles, fever, severely ill → immediate IV acyclovir.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Skin barrier defect","desc":"Filaggrin mutations reduce transepidermal water retention → dry, vulnerable skin that allows allergens through."},{"title":"Th2 inflammation","desc":"IL-4 and IL-13 activate IgE production and worsen barrier dysfunction — basis for dupilumab (IL-4Rα antagonist)."},{"title":"Prurigo nodularis","desc":"Chronic scratching leads to hyperpigmented, raised nodules — extreme form of chronic eczema."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Allergic contact dermatitis","desc":"Pattern follows exposure (belt, watch), patch test positive."},{"label":"Inverse psoriasis","desc":"In skin folds, sharply demarcated, barely itchy."},{"label":"Scabies","desc":"Intense nocturnal pruritus, interdigital + wrists, group outbreaks — exclude in worsening eczema."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Intensive emollients (several times per day) + topical corticosteroids.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Moderate","detail":"Tacrolimus or pimecrolimus (calcineurin inhibitors) — especially face/folds."},{"naam":"Severe","detail":"Dupilumab (IL-4/IL-13 blocker) or tralokinumab; cyclosporine as bridge."},{"naam":"Phototherapy","detail":"Narrowband UVB in extensive eczema."}]},{"_i":308,"q":"65-year-old woman, asymmetric pigmented lesion on the back, irregular borders, multiple colors (brown, black, red). Growing over recent months. Diagnosis to exclude?","a":["Melanoma","Seborrheic keratosis","Blue nevus","Dermatofibroma"],"ex":"ABCDE in pigmented lesion: Asymmetry, irregular Border, multiple colors (Color), Diameter >6 mm, Evolution. Always excision biopsy — never shave biopsy.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Melanoma: aggressive skin carcinoma of melanocytes. Prognosis determined by Breslow thickness (invasion depth in mm) and presence of lymph node metastases.","wiki_redflag":"Amelanotic melanoma (non-pigmented) lacks all ABCDE features — often missed as an 'ordinary pimple'. High mortality due to late diagnosis.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"BRAF-V600E mutation","desc":"Present in ~50% of melanomas → targeted therapy with BRAF inhibitors (vemurafenib) + MEK inhibitors (trametinib)."},{"title":"Immunotherapy","desc":"Anti-PD-1 (nivolumab, pembrolizumab) and anti-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab) have revolutionarily improved survival in metastatic melanoma."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Seborrheic keratosis","desc":"'Stuck-on' appearance, matte surface, no melanocytes — benign."},{"label":"Blue nevus","desc":"Uniform blue-gray, benign."},{"label":"Spitz nevus","desc":"In children, red or pink — histologically difficult to distinguish from melanoma → excision."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Excision with clear margin (0.5-2 cm depending on Breslow thickness) — as soon as possible.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Sentinel lymph node biopsy","detail":"With Breslow thickness ≥0.8 mm to detect lymph node metastases."},{"naam":"Stage III/IV","detail":"Immunotherapy (anti-PD-1 ± anti-CTLA-4) and/or BRAF/MEK inhibitors with BRAF mutation."},{"naam":"Adjuvant therapy","detail":"Nivolumab or pembrolizumab in high-risk resected melanoma."}]},{"_i":309,"q":"30-year-old woman, severe acne on face and chest, not responding to topical treatment and antibiotics. She wants to become pregnant. Which treatment is now NOT indicated?","a":["Isotretinoin (Roaccutane)","Topical azelaic acid","Topical clindamycin","Oral doxycycline"],"ex":"Isotretinoin is strongly teratogenic — absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy or pregnancy wish without strict contraception protocol.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid): most effective acne treatment — acts on all four pathomechanisms (sebaceous gland hypertrophy, comedones, Cutibacterium acnes, inflammation).","wiki_redflag":"Isotretinoin + pregnancy = severe congenital malformations (skull, heart, central nervous system). Always two forms of contraception mandatory — also when in doubt.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Adverse effects","desc":"Dry lips, skin and mucous membranes (almost everyone), hypertriglyceridemia, liver function abnormalities."},{"title":"Combination danger","desc":"Isotretinoin + tetracycline → increased risk of pseudotumor cerebri (raised intracranial pressure) — combination forbidden."},{"title":"Psychiatry","desc":"FDA black box for suicidality, but causal relationship not proven — monitor nonetheless."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Oral doxycycline","desc":"Moderate-severe acne, maximum 3 months (resistance risk)."},{"label":"Hormonal contraceptives","desc":"Cyproterone acetate/ethinylestradiol: anti-androgen effect, effective in women."},{"label":"Benzoyl peroxide","desc":"Bactericidal, resistance-free — safe during pregnancy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Mild: topical retinoids + benzoyl peroxide.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Moderate","detail":"Add topical or oral antibiotic (max 3 months)."},{"naam":"Severe/refractory","detail":"Isotretinoin — mandatory with contraception and monthly monitoring (liver, lipids, pregnancy test)."}]},{"_i":310,"q":"72-year-old man, sinister skin lesion on lower lip, raised ulcerating border, slowly growing over months. Smoker, worked outdoors a lot. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)","Basal cell carcinoma","Melanoma","Keratoacanthoma"],"ex":"Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC): ulcerating lesion with raised border in older adults with sun damage. Metastatic risk 2-5% — higher on lip and ear.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Non-melanoma skin carcinomas: basal cell carcinoma (BCC, 75%) and squamous cell carcinoma (SCC, 20%). BCC rarely metastasizes but is locally destructive; SCC can give regional lymph node metastases.","wiki_redflag":"SCC on lip, ear or in immunocompromised patients: metastatic risk up to 15-20% — always palpate lymph nodes and low threshold for imaging.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"BCC features","desc":"Pearly borders, telangiectasias, central ulceration ('rodent ulcer') — preference for nose, cheeks, eyelids."},{"title":"SCC features","desc":"Keratotic ulcer with undermined border, on background of actinic keratosis (precursor lesion)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Keratoacanthoma","desc":"Rapidly growing (weeks), crater-shaped with horn plug — histologically difficult to distinguish from SCC → always excise."},{"label":"Melanoma","desc":"Pigmented, ABCDE criteria."},{"label":"Merkel cell carcinoma","desc":"Rare, aggressive neuroendocrine carcinoma — high metastatic risk, rapidly growing."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"SCC: excision with clear margin (>5 mm). Check lymph node stations.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Mohs surgery","detail":"For SCC head-neck or recurrence — tissue-sparing, lowest recurrence risk."},{"naam":"Actinic keratoses (precursor)","detail":"Topical 5-fluorouracil (5-FU), imiquimod or cryotherapy."},{"naam":"Metastatic SCC","detail":"Cemiplimab (anti-PD-1 immunotherapy)."}]},{"_i":311,"q":"35-year-old man, vesicular lesions along left flank, pain that had already existed for 3 days before the rash, burning sensation. Recent stress. Diagnosis?","a":["Herpes zoster (shingles)","Herpes simplex","Varicella (primary infection)","Allergic contact dermatitis"],"ex":"Herpes zoster: VZV reactivation with prodromal pain 2-3 days before rash. Unilateral, dermatomal, never crosses the midline.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Shingles = reactivation of varicella-zoster virus (VZV) with reduced cellular immunity (old age, stress, immunosuppression, HIV). VZV remains latent in dorsal ganglia.","wiki_redflag":"Zoster ophthalmicus (V1 branch of trigeminal nerve, nasal tip vesicles = Hutchinson sign): risk of corneal ulceration and visual damage — immediate ophthalmology consult.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)","desc":"Burning, persistent pain ≥3 months after skin healing — more common in older adults and with severe initial pain."},{"title":"PHN risk factors","desc":"Age >60 years, severe prodromal pain, immunosuppression."},{"title":"PHN treatment","desc":"Gabapentin or pregabalin, tricyclic antidepressant (amitriptyline), capsaicin patch or opioids."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Herpes simplex","desc":"Labialis or genitalis, smaller vesicles, recurrence in same site, not dermatomal."},{"label":"Varicella primary infection","desc":"Disseminated vesicles across whole body, not dermatomal, especially children."},{"label":"Allergic contact dermatitis","desc":"Can also be unilateral but itching predominant, no prodromal pain."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acyclovir 800 mg 5×/day for 7 days, or valacyclovir 1000 mg 3×/day — start within 72 hours after rash.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Analgesia","detail":"NSAID + paracetamol; add opioids with severe pain."},{"naam":"Vaccination prevention","detail":"Shingrix (non-live, 2 doses, more effective) — also in immunocompromised people."}]},{"_i":312,"q":"Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin carcinoma and has a high risk of metastasis.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE: BCC is the most common skin carcinoma (75%), but metastasizes extremely rarely (<0.1%). It is locally destructive, however.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Skin carcinoma frequency: basal cell carcinoma (BCC) > squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) > melanoma. BCC subtypes: nodular (most frequent), superficial, pigmented and morpheaform (infiltrative).","wiki_redflag":"Morpheaform BCC: flat, hard, poorly demarcated — high chance of incomplete excision. With bone invasion or perineural growth: Mohs surgery and possibly radiotherapy.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"PTCH1 mutation","desc":"Sonic Hedgehog signaling pathway disrupted → unrestrained basal cell proliferation. UV radiation is the main mutagenic factor."},{"title":"Gorlin syndrome","desc":"Hereditary PTCH1 mutation → multiple BCCs at young age + skeletal abnormalities + medulloblastoma."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Nodular BCC","desc":"Pearly, telangiectasias, central ulcer — most common subtype."},{"label":"Morpheaform BCC","desc":"Flat, hard, poorly demarcated — higher chance of recurrence after excision."},{"label":"SCC","desc":"Keratotic ulcer, raised border — does have metastatic risk."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Excision with clear margin (3-5 mm). Mohs surgery in high-risk location (face, H-zone).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Superficial BCC","detail":"Imiquimod cream, 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) or photodynamic therapy (PDT) as alternative to excision."},{"naam":"Unresectable BCC","detail":"Vismodegib (Sonic Hedgehog inhibitor) or radiotherapy."}]},{"_i":313,"q":"A positive VDRL/RPR test is diagnostic for active syphilis.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE: VDRL/RPR is a screening test with low specificity — always confirm with treponemal test (TPHA or FTA-ABS).","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Syphilis diagnostics: (1) Screening test: VDRL or RPR (non-treponemal, becomes negative after treatment, titer = activity measure). (2) Confirmation test: TPHA or FTA-ABS (treponemal, remains positive for life).","wiki_redflag":"Neurosyphilis can occur at any stage — think of altered behavior, psychiatric symptoms or meningismus in syphilis. Requires lumbar puncture and IV penicillin (not IM).","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Syphilis stages","desc":"Primary: painless chancre. Secondary: rash on palms/soles, condylomata lata, generalized lymphadenopathy. Latent: no symptoms. Tertiary: gumma, cardiovascular syphilis, neurosyphilis."},{"title":"Transmission","desc":"Sexual contact or vertical (mother-child → congenital syphilis)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Chancroid (Haemophilus ducreyi)","desc":"Painful genital ulcer — unlike syphilis (painless)."},{"label":"Genital herpes","desc":"Painful vesicles, recurrent."},{"label":"Lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV)","desc":"Chlamydia trachomatis L1-3, inguinal nodes, proctitis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Primary/secondary/latent <1 year: benzylpenicillin 2.4 million IU IM once.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Tertiary/neurosyphilis/latent >1 year","detail":"Benzylpenicillin IV for 10-14 days."},{"naam":"Penicillin allergy","detail":"Doxycycline (not in neurosyphilis — poor CSF penetration)."},{"naam":"Congenital syphilis","detail":"Neonatal benzylpenicillin."}]},{"_i":314,"q":"Scabies is caused by a fungus and is treatable with a topical antifungal cream.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE: Scabies = mite (Sarcoptes scabiei), not fungus. Treatment: permethrin 5% cream or oral ivermectin — antifungal cream has no effect.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Scabies: intense nocturnal itching due to hypersensitivity to the mite Sarcoptes scabiei and its feces. Preferred locations: interdigital spaces, wrists, nipple areola, genitals.","wiki_redflag":"Norwegian (crusted) scabies in immunocompromised patients: millions of mites, hyperkeratotic plaques, extremely contagious — isolation and ivermectin mandatory.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Pathogenesis","desc":"Sarcoptes scabiei digs burrows in superficial skin to lay eggs → hypersensitivity reaction to mite and feces → intense nocturnal pruritus."},{"title":"Transmission","desc":"Direct prolonged skin contact (sexual, household). Treat all household members and close contacts simultaneously."},{"title":"Bedding","desc":"Wash at ≥60°C or 72 hours in plastic bag (mites survive max. 2-3 days without host)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Eczema","desc":"Also itching, but no burrows and not typically interdigital or nocturnal."},{"label":"Urticaria","desc":"Transient wheals, not dermatomal."},{"label":"Tinea pedis","desc":"Interdigital fungus — white maceration, no nocturnal itching."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Permethrin 5% cream: first choice — whole body (neck through feet), 8-12 hours, repeat after 1 week.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Ivermectin 200 μg/kg orally","detail":"Alternative or in crusted scabies — two doses at 2-week interval."},{"naam":"Itching","detail":"Antihistamines — itching can persist for weeks due to dead mite remnants (not a sign of treatment failure)."}]},{"_i":315,"q":"Which feature distinguishes urticaria (hives) from angioedema in an allergic reaction?","a":["Urticaria: superficial itchy wheals in the dermis; angioedema: deep swelling in the subcutis without itching","Urticaria always causes shortness of breath, angioedema never","Urticaria is IgE-mediated, angioedema is always C1q inhibitor deficiency","Angioedema gives red lesions, urticaria gives pale lesions"],"ex":"Urticaria = itchy dermal wheals (<24 h per lesion). Angioedema = deeper subcutaneous swelling, less itchy — dangerous with laryngeal involvement.","dl":"Dermatology","wiki_kern":"Anaphylaxis = systemic IgE-mediated reaction: urticaria + angioedema + bronchospasm + hypotension. Treatment: adrenaline 0.3-0.5 mg IM (lateral thigh) + O2 + lying with legs elevated.","wiki_redflag":"Angioedema of the larynx: stridor, hoarseness, drooling → life-threatening airway obstruction. Immediate IM adrenaline — do not wait for antihistamines.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"IgE-mediated (type I)","desc":"Allergen → IgE on mast cells → histamine release → vasodilation and increased permeability → wheals (dermis) or swelling (subcutis)."},{"title":"Hereditary angioedema (HAE)","desc":"C1 inhibitor deficiency → bradykinin excess → swelling without itching and without urticaria. Does NOT respond to antihistamines or adrenaline."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hereditary angioedema (HAE)","desc":"No itching, no urticaria, recurrent, family history, not responding to antihistamines."},{"label":"Chronic spontaneous urticaria","desc":">6 weeks, cause unclear — daily antihistamine, if insufficient response omalizumab."},{"label":"ACE inhibitor angioedema","desc":"Bradykinin-mediated (not IgE) — also not responding to antihistamines. Stop ACE inhibitor immediately."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Anaphylaxis: adrenaline 0.3-0.5 mg IM lateral thigh — repeat after 5 min if needed.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Antihistamines + corticosteroids","detail":"Adjunct after adrenaline — delay late-phase reaction but are not primary treatment in anaphylaxis."},{"naam":"HAE treatment","detail":"Icatibant (bradykinin B2 antagonist), C1 inhibitor concentrate; prophylaxis: lanadelumab."}]},{"_i":316,"q":"45-year-old woman, symmetrical pain and swelling of the MCP and PIP joints, morning stiffness >1 hour, fatigue, RF and anti-CCP positive. Diagnosis?","a":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Reactive arthritis"],"ex":"RA: symmetrical joint inflammation MCP/PIP/wrists, morning stiffness >1 h, RF and anti-CCP positive. DIP joints are spared in RA (unlike osteoarthritis). Anti-CCP is more specific (95%) than RF (70-80%) for RA.","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"RA: autoimmune synovitis through T- and B-cell activation → TNF-α/IL-6/IL-1 production → synovial pannus → cartilage erosion → bone destruction. Prevalence 1%, women 3×.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Anti-CCP (anti-citrullinated protein antibodies): pathological → present years before symptoms, predicts erosive course."},{"title":"RF","desc":"non-specific (also in SjS, SLE, hepatitis C)."},{"title":"Erosions on X-ray","desc":"marginal erosions at the "bare area" of the joint."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Osteoarthritis","desc":"non-inflammatory, DIP>PIP, morning stiffness <30 min, no RF/CCP, Heberden nodes."},{"label":"SLE","desc":"butterfly rash, photosensitivity, ANA+, multiple organ involvement."},{"label":"Reactive arthritis","desc":"after infection (STD or intestinal infection), asymmetric, HLA-B27+."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Treat-to-target: remission as goal.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Methotrexate (MTX)","detail":"cornerstone (weekly + folic acid)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"MTX + biologics (anti-TNF: etanercept, adalimumab; IL-6 inhibitor: tocilizumab)."},{"naam":"JAK inhibitors","detail":"baricitinib, tofacitinib."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"NSAIDs + corticosteroids as bridge."}]},{"_i":317,"q":"35-year-old man, sudden severe pain and redness of the big toe (MTP-1 joint), at night. Serum uric acid 0.58 mmol/L, leukocytosis. Diagnosis?","a":["Gout","Septic arthritis","Pseudogout (CPPD)","Reactive arthritis"],"ex":"Gout: crystal arthropathy due to monosodium urate crystals. Classic: sudden arthritis MTP-1 (podagra) at night, red, warm, severely painful. Hyperuricemia is predisposing but the attack itself can occur with normal uric acid levels.","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"Gout attack: urate crystals → activation of NLRP3 inflammasome → IL-1β production → acute inflammation. Risk factors: hyperuricemia (purine-rich diet, alcohol, diuretics, renal insufficiency).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Diagnosis of gout attack","desc":"synovial fluid crystal analysis (needle-shaped, negatively birefringent under polarized light)."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Serum uric acid can be normal during attack."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Colchicine (start early), NSAID or corticosteroid for attack treatment."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Septic arthritis","desc":"also monoarthritis with red/hot joint but fever + ill + high leukocytes → immediate aspiration! Pseudogout (CPPD): calcium pyrophosphate crystals, positively birefringent, older, knee/wrist."},{"label":"Reactive arthritis","desc":"after infection, HLA-B27, Reiter's triad (arthritis+urethritis+conjunctivitis)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Attack: colchicine 0.5 mg 3×/day or NSAID or pred 30 mg.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Prevent recurrence","detail":"allopurinol (XO inhibitor) or febuxostat."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Target uric acid <0.36 mmol/L."},{"naam":"Lifestyle","detail":"less purine (red meat, organ meat), no alcohol (beer+spirits), drink plenty."}]},{"_i":318,"q":"28-year-old woman, butterfly-shaped rash on cheeks, photosensitivity, joint pain, proteinuria, ANA positive (titer 1:640), anti-dsDNA positive. Diagnosis?","a":["Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","Rheumatoid arthritis","Dermatomyositis","Sjögren syndrome"],"ex":"SLE: autoimmune disease with multiple organ involvement. ACR/EULAR criteria: butterfly rash, photosensitivity, serositis, kidney disease, neurological, hematological, immunological (anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, complement low). ANA positive in >95% of SLE.","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"SLE: mainly women of reproductive age (9:1 female:male), higher in African-American and Asian populations. Antinuclear antibodies (ANA): very sensitive (>95%) but not specific.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Lupus nephritis: immune complex deposition in glomeruli."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"complement activation."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"inflammation. Class III/IV: most severe form, treatment: hydroxychloroquine + MMF (mycophenolic acid) or cyclophosphamide + steroids. Biopsy for classification."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"RA","desc":"joints dominant, anti-CCP+, no butterfly rash."},{"label":"Dermatomyositis","desc":"rash + muscle weakness, heliotrope rash, CK elevated."},{"label":"SjS","desc":"dry eyes/mouth, anti-SSA/SSB+, milder systemic involvement."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Hydroxychloroquine: foundation for all SLE patients (reduces flares, cardiovascular protective).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Flares","detail":"corticosteroids."},{"naam":"Organ-threatening disease","detail":"MMF, cyclophosphamide, belimumab (anti-BAFF)."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Sun avoidance."}]},{"_i":319,"q":"30-year-old man, back pain >3 months, improves with movement and not with rest, morning stiffness >1 hour, HLA-B27 positive. MRI sacroiliac joints: edema. Diagnosis?","a":["Axial spondyloarthritis (ankylosing spondylitis)","Lumbar herniated nucleus pulposus","Rheumatoid arthritis","Fibromyalgia"],"ex":"Axial spondyloarthritis: inflammatory back pain (better with movement, not with rest), morning stiffness >1 h, onset <45 years, HLA-B27+ (90%), sacroiliitis on MRI (edema = active inflammation). Ankylosing spondylitis is the radiographic variant (HNP gives mechanical pain, worse with movement).","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"SpA group: axial SpA (AS), peripheral SpA, PsA (psoriatic arthritis), reactive arthritis, arthritis in IBD. Characteristic: HLA-B27 association, enthesitis (at tendon/ligament insertion), asymmetric peripheral arthritis, eye involvement (anterior uveitis).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Enthesitis","desc":"inflammation at osseotendinous junctions (Achilles tendons, ischial bone)."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"IL-17 dominant in spondyloarthritis."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab, ixekizumab) effective in axial SpA."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"Anti-TNF (etanercept, adalimumab) also first biological choice."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"HNP","desc":"mechanical pain, worse with movement and bending, improves with rest, dermatomal radiating pain."},{"label":"Fibromyalgia","desc":"diffuse pain, no inflammatory markers, MRI normal, normal HLA."},{"label":"RA","desc":"symmetrical peripheral, anti-CCP+, sacroiliitis not typical."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Step 1: NSAID maximum dose (diagnostic and therapeutic).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"biologic (anti-TNF or anti-IL-17)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"No DMARDs (MTX) in purely axial disease (not proven effective)."},{"naam":"Physiotherapy","detail":"essential (posture improvement)."}]},{"_i":320,"q":"70-year-old man, pain and stiffness in shoulders and hips, ESR 85 mm/h, CRP elevated. No muscle weakness. Rapid response to low-dose prednisolone. Diagnosis?","a":["Polymyalgia rheumatica (PMR)","Rheumatoid arthritis","Fibromyalgia","Polymyositis"],"ex":"PMR: older than 50 years (average 70), bilateral pain and stiffness in shoulders/hips, high ESR/CRP, no muscle weakness (distinguishes from polymyositis), dramatic response to low-dose prednisolone (15-20 mg). Associated with temporal arteritis (GCA).","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"PMR is associated with GCA (giant cell arteritis) in 10-15% of patients. Always exclude temporal arteritis: headache, jaw claudication, visual changes, blindness (AION).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"PMR pathology","desc":"synovitis of shoulder bursae and tenosynovitis of hip tendon structures."},{"title":"PET-CT","desc":"increased uptake in bursae and tenosynovial tissue in PMR."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"IL-6 elevated (tocilizumab effective in GCA)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Polymyositis","desc":"proximal muscle weakness (climbing stairs, raising arms), elevated CK, myopathic EMG."},{"label":"RA","desc":"distal joints, RF/CCP positive."},{"label":"Fibromyalgia","desc":"normal laboratory values."},{"label":"Hypothyroidism","desc":"also stiffness but normal ESR, elevated TSH."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Prednisolone 15-20 mg/day → rapid symptom relief (diagnostic).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Taper over 1-2 years."},{"naam":"GCA","detail":"prednisolone 60 mg."},{"naam":"Relapse during taper","detail":"return to previous dose."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Methotrexate as steroid-sparing in recurrence."},{"naam":"Tocilizumab","detail":"in GCA (IL-6 inhibitor)."}]},{"_i":321,"q":"50-year-old woman, dry eyes and mouth for years, painful joints, anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La) antibodies positive. Diagnosis?","a":["Primary Sjögren syndrome","SLE","Rheumatoid arthritis","Sarcoidosis"],"ex":"Primary Sjögren: dry eyes (xerophthalmia, Schirmer test <5 mm/5 min) + dry mouth (xerostomia) + anti-SSA/SSB positive. Secondary Sjögren: associated with RA or SLE. Complications: lymphoma (B-cell, 5× increased risk), interstitial lung disease, peripheral neuropathy.","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"Sjögren: autoimmune exocrine gland inflammation → dry eyes, mouth, skin, vaginal mucosa. ANA positive (>90%), anti-SSA/SSB (specific).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Lymphoma risk","desc":"parotid swelling, low C4, cryoglobulinemia, skin vasculitis increase risk."},{"title":"Monitoring","desc":"annual parotid swelling, lymphadenopathy."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"B-cell lymphomas, especially MALT type."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"SLE","desc":"multiple organ involvement, anti-dsDNA+, butterfly rash."},{"label":"RA","desc":"erosive arthritis, anti-CCP+, but SjS overlap frequent."},{"label":"Sarcoidosis","desc":"also dry eyes/mouth but ACE elevated, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Eyes: artificial tears, cyclosporine eye drops, punctal occlusion.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Mouth","detail":"stimulation of saliva flow (chewing gum, pilocarpine), good oral care (caries prophylaxis)."},{"naam":"Systemic","detail":"hydroxychloroquine, NSAID."},{"naam":"Severe organ disease","detail":"steroids, rituximab."}]},{"_i":322,"q":"25-year-old man, arthritis knee, urethral discharge 2 weeks earlier, conjunctivitis, skin lesions on palms and soles. HLA-B27 positive. Diagnosis?","a":["Reactive arthritis (Reiter syndrome)","Septic arthritis","Gout","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"ex":"Reactive arthritis (Reiter syndrome): arthritis + urethritis + conjunctivitis after sexual infection (Chlamydia) or gastrointestinal infection (Salmonella, Yersinia). HLA-B27 strongly associated. Keratoderma blennorrhagicum = skin lesions on palms/soles.","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"Reactive arthritis: peripheral asymmetric oligoarthritis after infection 1-4 weeks earlier. Infection itself already resolved.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"HLA-B27","desc":"risk allele for SpA group."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Molecular mimicry or faulty antigen presentation by HLA-B27 as pathomechanism."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Arthritis is autoimmune, not direct infection of the joint."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Septic arthritis","desc":"always bacterial, direct joint infection, fever + high leukocytes."},{"label":"Gout","desc":"MTP-1, night, serum uric acid high."},{"label":"Gonococcal arthritis","desc":"STD, septic, gram stain of joint aspirate."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"NSAID for arthritis (symptomatic).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Antibiotics if infection still active."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Most episodes self-limiting (3-6 months)."},{"naam":"HLA-B27+","detail":"20-30% chance of chronic course or axial SpA."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Sulfasalazine in chronic recurrent cases."}]},{"_i":323,"q":"Methotrexate in rheumatoid arthritis must always be combined with folic acid supplementation.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase → folic acid deficiency as adverse effect (stomatitis, nausea, hepatotoxicity, macrocytosis). Folic acid 5 mg 1×/week (day after MTX) or 1 mg daily significantly reduces adverse effects without reducing efficacy.","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"MTX mechanism in RA: polyglutamates inhibit ATIC (AICAR transformylase) → adenosine accumulation → anti-inflammatory effect. Also folate antagonist → adverse effects.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Absolute contraindications MTX","desc":"pregnancy (teratogenic, abortive), renal insufficiency eGFR <30, liver disease (liver cirrhosis), alcoholism, extensive lung disease."},{"title":"Relative contraindications","desc":"COPD, obesity, diabetes."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Leflunomide","desc":"also DMARD, folate antagonist but via a different enzyme (DHODH)."},{"label":"Sulfasalazine","desc":"fewer adverse effects, less effective than MTX."},{"label":"Hydroxychloroquine","desc":"mildest DMARD, no liver monitoring needed, safe in pregnancy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":324,"q":"With suspected septic arthritis, an X-ray of the joint must first be made before joint aspiration is performed.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. With suspected septic arthritis, immediate joint aspiration (arthrocentesis) is the priority: for diagnosis (Gram stain and culture) and therapeutic (pressure reduction). X-ray does not show septic arthritis in the early stage and must not delay aspiration.","dl":"Rheumatology","wiki_kern":"Septic arthritis: medical emergency. Bacteria destroy cartilage within 24-48 h through bacterial enzymes and inflammatory response.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Most frequent pathogens","desc":"S. aureus (50%), streptococci, gonococci (younger sexually active patients)."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Hematogenous (from bloodstream) or direct inoculation (after puncture, surgery)."},{"title":"Joint fluid laboratory","desc":"leukocytes >50,000/μL, PMN >90%, glucose low."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Gout","desc":"crystal arthropathy, also red/hot but no bacteria in joint fluid."},{"label":"Reactive arthritis","desc":"sterile joint fluid, trigger infection elsewhere."},{"label":"Hemarthrosis","desc":"blood in joint after trauma."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"IV antibiotics (S. aureus: flucloxacillin; MRSA: vancomycin).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Drainage","detail":"repeated aspirations or surgical arthrotomy in hip/shoulder."},{"naam":"Duration AB","detail":"4-6 weeks."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Physiotherapy after infection treated."}]}]}
